    gaps:{title:"Past continuous", bank:["was standing","was blowing","were shaking","was waiting","was looking","were firing"], items:[["Pip ___ among the gravestones when the convict appeared.","was standing"],["A cold wind ___ across the marsh that afternoon.","was blowing"],["Pip's hands ___ as he answered the man's questions.","were shaking"],["The convict ___ for food when Pip came back.","was waiting"],["Pip ___ at the iron on the man's leg.","was looking"],["That night the guns from the prison ships ___ across the marsh.","were firing"]]},
    think:{quote:"The convict seizes Pip among the gravestones and threatens him, but underneath the threats he is desperate, cold and starving.", question:"Why might Dickens make us feel sorry for the convict even though he frightens Pip?", options:["Because he is really a gentle man.","Because his fear and hunger show he is more pitiful than dangerous.","Because Pip's family already knows him."], answer:1, note:"Great Expectations often shows frightening people as also suffering people — watch for this pattern again with Magwitch later in the story."},
