  { n:2, title:"Christmas and the hunt", unit:2,
    sum:"It is Christmas Day, and Pip sits in agony through dinner, waiting to be caught for the stolen pie. Soldiers arrive with handcuffs to mend, and Joe, Pip and Mr Wopsle join the hunt for two escaped convicts across the marsh. The men are found fighting in a ditch; Pip's convict confesses that he stole the food himself, to protect Pip, and Joe generously says the man was welcome to it.",
    vocab:[["GUILTY","feeling that you have done something wrong"],["AGONY","very great physical or mental pain"],["HANDCUFFS","metal rings joined by a chain, put on a prisoner's wrists"],["SOLDIER","a member of an army"],["SERGEANT","an officer of middle rank in the army"],["DITCH","a long, narrow channel dug in the ground, often full of water"],["GENEROUS","willing to give more than is expected"],["CAPTURE","the act of catching a person, especially by force"],["ESCAPE","to get free from a place where you are kept prisoner"],["CONFESS","to admit that you have done something wrong"],["GRATEFUL","feeling thankful for something somebody has done"],["POMPOUS","too serious and self-important"]],
    comp:[["Who comes to Christmas dinner besides the family?",["Miss Havisham","Mr Wopsle, Mr Hubble and Uncle Pumblechook","Estella","The sergeant"],1,"A typical crowded Victorian Christmas table."],["Why is Pip in agony during dinner?",["He is ill","He fears being caught for the stolen pie","He dislikes the food","He is bored"],1,"Every mention of the pantry makes his heart stop."],["Why does the sergeant come to the house?",["To arrest Pip","To ask Joe to mend some handcuffs","To eat dinner","To buy iron"],1,"A pair of handcuffs need a link mended before the hunt."],["What does Pip learn from the sergeant?",["Nothing important","That two convicts have escaped and soldiers will hunt them","That his pie has been found","That Christmas is cancelled"],1,"News that terrifies and relieves him at the same time."],["Where are the two convicts finally found?",["At the inn","Fighting in a ditch on the marsh","On a boat","In the churchyard"],1,"Rolling over each other in the mud like fighting dogs."],["What does Pip's convict tell the soldiers?",["Nothing","That he stole the food from the blacksmith's himself","That Pip is guilty","That he is innocent"],1,"He protects Pip without ever looking at him."],["How does Joe react to the confession?",["He is angry","He says the man was welcome to the food","He denies it happened","He laughs"],1,"A moment of quiet generosity in a hard, cold field."],["How does Pip feel as the convicts are taken away?",["Only relieved","Sorry rather than afraid, for the first time","Angry","Bored"],1,"His feelings about the convict are starting to change."]],
    tf:[["Pip enjoys Christmas dinner completely.",false,"He suffers in agony, waiting to be caught."],["The sergeant comes about the stolen pie.",false,"He comes to have handcuffs mended."],["Two convicts have escaped from the prison ships.",true,"That is why the soldiers are searching the marsh."],["The convicts are found fighting in a ditch.",true,"Rolling over each other, covered in mud."],["Pip's convict blames Pip for the theft.",false,"He confesses to stealing the food himself."],["Joe is angry at the convict for stealing food.",false,"He says the man was welcome to it."]],
    rw:[["Uncle Pumblechook is described as pompous.",0,"Right."],["The sergeant asks Joe to mend a pair of handcuffs.",0,"Right."],["Mrs Joe finds the pie is missing during dinner.",2,"Doesn't say — Pip fears this, but we are not told it happens."],["Joe joins the soldiers in the hunt across the marsh.",0,"Right."],["The two convicts are found sitting calmly together.",1,"Wrong — they are found fighting in a ditch."],["Pip's convict says he stole the food himself.",0,"Right — to protect Pip."],["Joe says the convict was welcome to the food.",0,"Right."],["The convicts escape again after being caught.",1,"Wrong — they are taken back to the prison ship."]],
    halves:[["Pip sits through Christmas dinner","in absolute agony over the missing pie."],["A sergeant arrives with soldiers","asking Joe to mend a pair of handcuffs."],["Joe learns that two convicts","have escaped from the prison ships in the river."],["Joe, Pip and Wopsle","join the soldiers in the hunt across the marsh."],["The two convicts are found","fighting each other in a muddy ditch."],["Pip's convict confesses","that he stole the food from the forge himself."],["Joe quietly says","that the poor man was welcome to it."],["As the boat carries the convicts away,","Pip feels sorry for the first time, not afraid."]],
    odd:[[["sergeant","soldier","officer","farmer"],3,"A farmer has no military role."],[["ditch","trench","gutter","hill"],3,"A hill is high ground, not a low, wet channel."],[["guilty","ashamed","responsible","innocent"],3,"Innocent is the opposite of guilty."],[["confess","admit","deny","own up"],2,"Deny is the opposite of confessing."],[["generous","kind","giving","selfish"],3,"Selfish is the opposite of generous."]],
    gaps:{title:"Modal verbs", bank:["must","would","had to","might","should","could"], items:[["Pip felt he ___ be discovered at any moment during dinner.","must"],["He knew Mrs Joe ___ notice the missing pie eventually.","would"],["The sergeant said the handcuffs ___ be mended before they left.","had to"],["Pip thought the soldiers ___ have come for the stolen pie instead.","might"],["Joe felt that nobody ___ be blamed for stealing when they were starving.","should"],["Pip wondered if his convict ___ escape before the soldiers found him.","could"]]},
    think:{quote:"With the soldiers already searching the marsh, Pip's convict stands up and confesses to a theft that was really Pip's, without ever once looking at the boy.", question:"Why do you think the convict protects Pip like this?", options:["Because he wants to seem innocent himself.","Because Pip's small act of kindness has meant more to him than anyone realises.","Because he is trying to trick the soldiers."], answer:1, note:"This quiet act of loyalty is the seed of everything the convict will do for Pip later in the story."},
    writing:[{task:"Describe the moment the soldiers found the two convicts fighting in the ditch.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Describe the sound and the darkness of the marsh","Describe what the soldiers saw in the ditch","Say what happened next"],starters:["We crossed the dark marsh with lanterns, and then...","In the ditch we saw...","The soldiers pulled...","Afterwards..."]},{task:"Why does Pip's convict confess to a crime he did not really commit alone? Give your opinion.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Say what the convict actually did","Say what this protects Pip from","Give your own idea about his reasons"],starters:["The convict tells the soldiers that...","This protects Pip because...","I think he does this because...","It shows that..."]}]},
  { n:3, title:"Satis House", unit:3,
    sum:"Uncle Pumblechook takes Pip to play at Satis House, the strange mansion of the rich Miss Havisham, who has worn the same yellowed wedding dress for years among stopped clocks and a rotten bride-cake. Her cold, beautiful adopted daughter Estella mocks Pip as a common boy with coarse hands and thick boots, and he cries with shame for the first time about his own home. Back at the forge he invents wild lies about the visit, but later confesses the truth to Joe, who tells him that lies are no way to become uncommon.",
    vocab:[["MANSION","a very large and impressive house"],["WEDDING","the ceremony at which two people are married"],["CANDLE","a stick of wax with a wick, burned to give light"],["CLOCK","an instrument that shows what time it is"],["COMMON","ordinary; not special or of high social class"],["COARSE","rough in texture, not smooth or fine"],["MOCK","to laugh at somebody unkindly, making fun of them"],["ASHAMED","feeling guilty or embarrassed about something"],["PRIDE","a feeling of your own importance or dignity"],["INVENT","to create something, especially a story, that is not true"],["ROTTEN","decayed and spoiled, especially through age"],["CRUEL","causing pain or suffering to others deliberately"]],
    comp:[["Why does Pip go to Satis House?",["To buy something","Miss Havisham wants a boy to come and play","To deliver a letter","To work there"],1,"Pumblechook arranges it, delighted by the connection to a rich lady."],["What is strange about the room where Miss Havisham sits?",["It is very bright","Every clock has stopped at the same time","It is full of flowers","It is outside"],1,"Twenty minutes to nine, and the curtains never open."],["What is Miss Havisham wearing?",["A black dress","An old, yellowed wedding dress","A soldier's uniform","Modern clothes"],1,"She has worn it since her wedding day, long ago."],["What does Estella say about Pip?",["That he is clever","That he has coarse hands and thick boots","That he is handsome","That he is rich"],1,"Her mocking words wound him more than any blow."],["How does Pip feel after Estella's comments?",["Proud","Ashamed of his home for the first time","Angry at Miss Havisham","Amused"],1,"He cries behind the brewery wall before anyone can see him."],["What does Pip tell Pumblechook about the visit?",["The truth","A wild, invented story","Nothing at all","That he was sent away"],1,"Pride will not let him admit what really happened."],["Who does Pip finally tell the truth to?",["Mrs Joe","Joe","Estella","Miss Havisham"],1,"He cannot carry the lie alone any longer."],["What does Joe say about lying?",["That it is clever","That it is no way to become uncommon","That everyone does it","That Pip should lie again"],1,"Joe's simple honesty is the opposite of what Pip has just done."]],
    tf:[["Miss Havisham lives at Satis House.",true,"A gloomy old mansion behind a locked gate."],["Miss Havisham's clocks are all working perfectly.",false,"Every one of them stopped years ago."],["Estella is kind and welcoming to Pip.",false,"She mocks his hands and his boots."],["Pip cries after his visit to Satis House.",true,"Behind the brewery wall, out of sight."],["Pip tells Pumblechook exactly what happened.",false,"He invents a wild, untrue story instead."],["Joe approves of Pip's lies.",false,"He says lies lead nowhere good."]],
    rw:[["Miss Havisham's wedding dress has turned yellow with age.",0,"Right — she has worn it for many years."],["The wedding-cake on the table is fresh.",1,"Wrong — it is rotten and full of holes."],["Estella calls Pip's hands coarse.",0,"Right — and his boots thick."],["Pip has always been ashamed of his home.",1,"Wrong — Estella's words teach him shame for the first time."],["Pip invents a story about gold plates and velvet coaches.",0,"Right — to hide the truth from his pride."],["Miss Havisham tells Estella to break hearts when she grows up.",0,"Right — words Pip does not yet understand."],["Joe is angry with Pip for lying.",2,"Doesn't say — he speaks quietly, not angrily."],["Pip plays cards with Estella.",0,"Right — and she mocks him while they play."]],
    halves:[["Miss Havisham has worn the same wedding dress","since her wedding day, many years before."],["Every clock in the house","stopped at twenty minutes to nine."],["Estella mocks Pip's hands and boots","and makes him ashamed of them for the first time."],["Miss Havisham tells Estella","to break hearts well when she grows up."],["Too proud to tell the truth,","Pip invents a wild story about the visit."],["That evening he confesses everything","to Joe, who listens without any anger."],["Joe tells Pip","that lies are no way to become uncommon."],["Pip lies awake","wondering if he wants more than a blacksmith's life."]],
    odd:[[["mansion","house","cottage","clock"],3,"A clock is an object, not a building."],[["coarse","rough","rude","smooth"],3,"Smooth is the opposite of coarse."],[["cruel","unkind","harsh","gentle"],3,"Gentle is the opposite of cruel."],[["ashamed","embarrassed","guilty","proud"],3,"Proud is closer to the opposite feeling."],[["invent","imagine","create","copy"],3,"To copy is not to invent something new."]],
    gaps:{title:"Comparatives", bank:["more ashamed","colder","more cruel","richer","thicker","stranger"], items:[["Pip felt ___ of his home than he had ever felt before.","more ashamed"],["Satis House was ___ inside than the coldest winter morning outside.","colder"],["Estella's laugh seemed ___ each time she looked at his boots.","more cruel"],["Miss Havisham was much ___ than anybody Pip had ever met.","richer"],["Pip's boots felt ___ and heavier under Estella's eyes.","thicker"],["The whole house seemed ___ than any place Pip had ever seen.","stranger"]]},
    think:{quote:"Estella laughs at Pip's coarse hands and thick boots, and for the first time in his life he feels ashamed of the home he has always loved.", question:"Why does Dickens make Pip's shame begin with something as small as a laugh?", options:["Because Pip is naturally a proud person.","Because other people's opinions can change how we see our own lives, even without a single unkind act.","Because Estella is more powerful than Miss Havisham."], answer:1, note:"Watch how this one afternoon changes the whole direction of Pip's story — his 'great expectations' begin here, in a single moment of shame."},
    writing:[{task:"Describe Miss Havisham's room and how it makes Pip feel.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Describe the stopped clocks and the wedding dress","Describe the old wedding-cake","Say what Pip feels as he looks around"],starters:["Every clock in the room...","In the middle of the table stood...","Miss Havisham herself...","Pip felt..."]},{task:"Was Pip right to invent a story for Pumblechook, or should he have told the truth? Give your opinion.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Say what the true story was","Say what Pip invented instead","Give your own opinion with a reason"],starters:["The true story was that...","Instead, Pip told Pumblechook that...","Joe later said that...","In my opinion, Pip..."]}]},
  { n:4, title:"The pale young gentleman", unit:4,
    sum:"Biddy helps Pip with his lessons, and a mysterious stranger at the Jolly Bargemen stirs his drink with Joe's own file and gives Pip two pounds. At Satis House Pip meets Miss Havisham's greedy relatives and fights a pale young gentleman in the garden, knocking him down, after which Estella lets him kiss her cheek. Pip pushes Miss Havisham's chair for months until she finally pays Joe twenty-five guineas to make Pip his apprentice, and Mrs Joe is furious at not being invited to hear the news herself.",
    vocab:[["STRANGER","a person you do not know"],["STIR","to move a liquid round and round with a spoon or similar object"],["APPRENTICE","a young person learning a trade from a skilled worker"],["GUINEA","an old British gold coin worth one pound and one shilling"],["RELATIVE","a member of your family"],["GREEDY","wanting more of something, especially money, than you need"],["GARDEN","an area of ground where flowers, plants or vegetables are grown"],["FIST","a hand closed tightly with the fingers bent into the palm"],["KNOCK","to hit somebody or something, especially so that it falls"],["CHEEK","the soft part of the face below each eye"],["FURIOUS","extremely angry"],["CREDIT","praise or approval for something you have done"]],
    comp:[["Who teaches Pip to read and write?",["Miss Havisham","Biddy","Mrs Joe","Estella"],1,"She shares everything she knows from the evening school."],["What does the stranger at the Jolly Bargemen do with his drink?",["Spills it","Stirs it with Joe's file","Refuses it","Shares it with Joe"],1,"Pip recognises the very file he stole years before."],["What does the stranger give Pip?",["A letter","Two pound notes","A file","Nothing"],1,"A strange, unexplained gift that links back to the convict."],["Who does Pip fight in the garden at Satis House?",["Herbert's cousin","A pale young gentleman","Pumblechook","A servant"],1,"The stranger seems oddly calm about being challenged and beaten."],["What happens after the fight?",["Pip is punished","Estella lets him kiss her cheek","Miss Havisham is angry","Nothing changes"],1,"A strange, unexpected reward for winning a fight nobody asked for."],["What does Pip do for Miss Havisham for months?",["Reads to her","Pushes her wheelchair round the garden","Cooks for her","Cleans the house"],1,"Round and round the overgrown garden, day after day."],["What does Miss Havisham finally pay for?",["A new house","Pip's apprenticeship to Joe","Pip's education","A wedding"],1,"Twenty-five guineas, handed to Joe in a small linen bag."],["Why is Mrs Joe furious?",["About the money","Because Pumblechook heard the news before she did","Because Pip fought someone","Because Joe was rude"],1,"She cares deeply about being told things first."]],
    tf:[["Biddy teaches Pip to read and write.",true,"Everything she knows from the evening school."],["The stranger at the inn stirs his drink with a spoon.",false,"He uses Joe's own file."],["Pip loses the fight in the garden.",false,"He knocks the pale young gentleman down three times."],["Estella lets Pip kiss her cheek after the fight.",true,"A strange kind of prize."],["Miss Havisham pays for Pip to become a gentleman immediately.",false,"She pays for his apprenticeship to Joe instead."],["Mrs Joe is pleased that Pumblechook hears the news first.",false,"She is absolutely furious about it."]],
    rw:[["Biddy helps at Mr Wopsle's great-aunt's evening school.",0,"Right."],["The stranger gives Pip two pound notes.",0,"Right."],["Camilla and Sarah Pocket are Miss Havisham's greedy relatives.",0,"Right — hoping for a share of her fortune."],["Pip has fought many times before.",1,"Wrong — he has never fought anybody in his life."],["Miss Havisham pays exactly twenty-five guineas for Pip's apprenticeship.",0,"Right."],["Joe talks directly to Miss Havisham throughout the visit.",1,"Wrong — he answers her questions through Pip instead."],["The pale young gentleman is angry after losing the fight.",2,"Doesn't say — Pip only tells us that he gave up."],["Pumblechook takes credit for arranging Pip's apprenticeship.",0,"Right — in front of the whole Jolly Bargemen."]],
    halves:[["Biddy teaches Pip everything she knows,","letter by letter, with all her heart."],["A stranger at the Jolly Bargemen","stirs his drink with Joe's own stolen file."],["At Satis House, greedy relatives","crowd round Miss Havisham hoping for her fortune."],["In the overgrown garden,","Pip fights and knocks down a pale young gentleman."],["As a strange reward for winning,","Estella allows Pip to kiss her cheek."],["For months Pip pushes","Miss Havisham's wheelchair round and round the garden."],["Miss Havisham finally pays Joe twenty-five guineas","to make Pip his formal apprentice."],["Mrs Joe is furious","that Pumblechook hears the good news before she does."]],
    odd:[[["stranger","visitor","guest","neighbour"],3,"A neighbour is somebody you already know well."],[["greedy","selfish","generous","grasping"],2,"Generous is the opposite of greedy."],[["furious","angry","enraged","calm"],3,"Calm is the opposite of furious."],[["garden","yard","field","ceiling"],3,"A ceiling is inside a building, not outdoors."],[["apprentice","trainee","learner","expert"],3,"An expert has already finished learning."]],
    gaps:{title:"Reported speech", bank:["was","would","had stirred","could","to say","what"], items:[["The stranger told Joe that he ___ a friend of a man from the marshes.","was"],["He said that he ___ never forget Pip's kindness.","would"],["Pip realised the stranger ___ his drink with Joe's own file.","had stirred"],["Joe said that he ___ not remember ever losing that file.","could"],["Miss Havisham told Joe ___ nothing to her directly.","to say"],["Pip wondered ___ the two pounds really meant.","what"]]},
    think:{quote:"A stranger stirs his rum with Joe's own file — the file Pip stole years before — and gives the boy two pounds without any explanation.", question:"What does this strange scene suggest to the reader?", options:["That the stranger is simply a generous traveller.","That somebody connected to the convict is still watching over Pip, though we do not yet know who or why.","That Joe has met the stranger before."], answer:1, note:"Dickens plants this mystery early and leaves it unanswered for many chapters — remember the file."},
    writing:[{task:"Describe the fight between Pip and the pale young gentleman in the garden.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Say how the fight began","Describe what happened","Say how Pip felt afterwards"],starters:["The pale young gentleman suddenly said...","I had never fought anybody, but...","After the third time he fell...","Afterwards I felt..."]},{task:"Why do you think Miss Havisham pays for Pip to become Joe's apprentice instead of something else? Give your opinion.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Say what she has been doing with Pip for months","Say what she finally decides","Give your own idea about her reasons"],starters:["For months Miss Havisham had Pip...","In the end she paid Joe...","I think she chose this because...","Another possible reason is..."]}]},
  { n:5, title:"Great expectations", unit:5,
    sum:"Pip grows increasingly unhappy as Joe's apprentice, ashamed of the forge and dreaming only of Estella. After a quarrel with Joe's surly journeyman Orlick, Mrs Joe is struck on the head one night with an old convict's leg-iron and never speaks properly again; Biddy comes to keep house for the family. Four years later the lawyer Mr Jaggers arrives with astonishing news: a secret benefactor has given Pip great expectations of a fortune, and he must leave at once for London to become a gentleman.",
    vocab:[["JOURNEYMAN","a skilled worker employed by another, not yet a master of the trade"],["QUARREL","an angry argument between two people"],["STRIKE","to hit somebody or something hard"],["SPEECHLESS","unable to speak, especially because of shock or injury"],["LAWYER","a person who is trained in the law"],["BENEFACTOR","a person who gives money to help somebody else"],["FORTUNE","a very large amount of money"],["GENTLEMAN","a man of good social position, especially in the past"],["SECRET","something kept hidden from other people"],["PATRONISE","to treat somebody as if they were less important than you"],["INHERIT","to receive money or property from somebody after they die or decide to give it"],["ASHAMED","feeling guilty or embarrassed about something"]],
    comp:[["How does Pip feel about his life as an apprentice?",["Completely happy","Increasingly discontented and ashamed of the forge","Proud of his work","Indifferent"],1,"He dreams only of becoming good enough for Estella."],["Who is Orlick?",["Pip's cousin","Joe's surly journeyman","A lawyer","A soldier"],1,"He quarrels constantly with Mrs Joe."],["What happens to Mrs Joe one night?",["She leaves home","She is struck on the head and never speaks properly again","She marries Orlick","She becomes rich"],1,"Struck with an old convict's iron leg-shackle."],["Who comes to keep house afterwards?",["Estella","Biddy","Miss Havisham","Pumblechook"],1,"She nurses Mrs Joe and runs the household."],["Who is Mr Jaggers?",["A soldier","A London lawyer","A blacksmith","A relative of Joe's"],1,"He arrives with astonishing, official news."],["What news does Jaggers bring Pip?",["That his father has died","That he has great expectations from a secret benefactor","That Joe is in debt","That Estella is engaged"],1,"A fortune and a life as a gentleman in London."],["Who does Pip assume his benefactor is?",["Jaggers himself","Miss Havisham","Orlick","Pumblechook"],1,"He has no real evidence, only hope."],["How does Pip treat Joe and Biddy before he leaves?",["With great respect","He patronises them, feeling superior","He ignores them completely","He asks their advice constantly"],1,"Something he is deeply ashamed of later."]],
    tf:[["Pip is completely happy working at the forge.",false,"He is increasingly discontented and ashamed of it."],["Orlick quarrels often with Mrs Joe.",true,"Neither of them will back down easily."],["Mrs Joe fully recovers and speaks normally again.",false,"She never speaks properly again after the attack."],["Biddy comes to keep house for the family.",true,"She nurses Mrs Joe and runs the household."],["Mr Jaggers tells Pip who his benefactor is.",false,"He warns Pip never to try to find out."],["Pip treats Joe and Biddy kindly before leaving.",false,"He patronises them, ashamed of it later."]],
    rw:[["Orlick works as a journeyman at Joe's forge.",0,"Right."],["Mrs Joe is struck with an iron leg-shackle.",0,"Right — one filed through on the marsh years before."],["The attacker is caught and punished.",1,"Wrong — nobody is ever charged with the attack."],["Pip suspects Orlick was responsible.",0,"Right — though he has no real proof."],["Mr Jaggers arrives exactly four years after the attack.",2,"Doesn't say — only that it is four quiet years later."],["Jaggers tells Pip he must go to London.",0,"Right — to be brought up as a gentleman."],["Pip is told the true name of his benefactor.",1,"Wrong — Jaggers warns him never to try to discover it."],["Pip assumes that Miss Havisham is his benefactor.",0,"Right — though he has no proof of this either."]],
    halves:[["Pip grows more and more discontented,","ashamed of the forge and dreaming only of Estella."],["Orlick and Mrs Joe","quarrel constantly, neither one willing to give way."],["One night somebody strikes Mrs Joe","with an old convict's iron leg-shackle."],["After the attack Mrs Joe","never speaks properly again."],["Biddy comes to live with the family","to nurse Mrs Joe and run the house."],["Four years later, Mr Jaggers arrives","with astonishing news about a secret benefactor."],["Pip is warned","never to try to discover who his benefactor is."],["Before he leaves for London,","Pip is ashamed to say he patronises Joe and Biddy."]],
    odd:[[["lawyer","judge","solicitor","farmer"],3,"A farmer has no legal profession."],[["fortune","wealth","riches","poverty"],3,"Poverty is the opposite of fortune."],[["quarrel","argument","dispute","agreement"],3,"Agreement is the opposite of quarrel."],[["secret","private","hidden","public"],3,"Public is the opposite of secret."],[["benefactor","supporter","donor","enemy"],3,"An enemy would not give you a fortune."]],
    gaps:{title:"Present perfect", bank:["has struck","has never spoken","have passed","has arrived","has decided","has given"], items:[["Somebody ___ Mrs Joe on the head with an iron shackle.","has struck"],["She ___ properly again since that terrible night.","has never spoken"],["Four quiet years ___ since the attack on Mrs Joe.","have passed"],["Mr Jaggers ___ with extraordinary news for Pip.","has arrived"],["A secret benefactor ___ to give Pip a fortune.","has decided"],["This benefactor ___ Pip the chance to become a gentleman.","has given"]]},
    think:{quote:"A secret benefactor has decided to give Pip a fortune, and the lawyer warns him never to try to discover who it is.", question:"Why do you think Pip is so sure that Miss Havisham is his secret benefactor?", options:["Because Jaggers tells him so directly.","Because he wants it to be true, since it would connect him to Estella.","Because Joe tells him it must be her."], answer:1, note:"Pip's own wishes, not the facts, are shaping what he believes — remember this moment when the truth is finally revealed much later in the story."},
    writing:[{task:"Describe the night Mrs Joe was attacked, and what happened afterwards.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Say where Joe and Pip were that evening","Describe what they found when they came home","Say how Mrs Joe changed afterwards"],starters:["That evening Joe and I were...","When we came home we found...","After the attack, Mrs Joe...","Biddy came to..."]},{task:"Imagine you are Pip on the night Mr Jaggers brings his news. Describe how you feel.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Say what Jaggers tells you","Say who you think your benefactor might be","Describe your feelings about leaving for London"],starters:["Mr Jaggers told me that...","I was sure that my benefactor must be...","About leaving for London, I felt...","I imagined that in London I would..."]}]},
  { n:6, title:"London", unit:6,
    sum:"In London, Pip visits Mr Jaggers's grim office near Newgate prison and meets his clerk Wemmick, who has a strange, closed post-office of a mouth. He lodges at Barnard's Inn with Herbert Pocket, the pale young gentleman from the garden, who renames him Handel and tells him how a swindler named Compeyson, helped by Miss Havisham's own half-brother, jilted her on her wedding morning. Pip studies with Mr Matthew Pocket alongside sulky, rich Bentley Drummle and friendly Startop, visits Wemmick's tiny castle at Walworth, and dines at Jaggers's house, where he notices the strange scars on the wrists of the housekeeper, Molly.",
    vocab:[["OFFICE","a room or building where business is done"],["CLERK","a person who works in an office, dealing with records and letters"],["LODGINGS","rooms rented in somebody else's house"],["SWINDLER","a person who cheats others out of money"],["JILT","to suddenly end a relationship with somebody you had promised to marry"],["BETRAYAL","an act of being disloyal to somebody who trusts you"],["SULKY","silent and bad-tempered because you are annoyed about something"],["HOUSEKEEPER","a person employed to manage a household"],["SCAR","a mark left on the skin after a wound has healed"],["POLITE","having good manners and behaving in a socially correct way"],["RESPECTABLE","considered to be correct, honest and socially acceptable"],["PRISONER","a person kept in prison as a punishment"]],
    comp:[["Where is Mr Jaggers's office?",["In Hammersmith","In a dark street near Newgate prison","At Satis House","In Walworth"],1,"Its walls are hung with death-masks of hanged criminals."],["What is unusual about Wemmick's mouth?",["He never speaks","It looks like a post-office slot","He always smiles","He whistles constantly"],1,"He seems to open it only to receive letters."],["Who does Pip lodge with in London?",["Mr Jaggers","Herbert Pocket, the pale young gentleman from the garden","Wemmick","Bentley Drummle"],1,"The very boy Pip once fought at Satis House."],["What new name does Herbert give Pip?",["Philip","Handel","Gentleman","Estella's friend"],1,"After a piece of music Herbert likes."],["Who jilted Miss Havisham on her wedding morning?",["Herbert Pocket","A swindler named Compeyson","Mr Jaggers","Bentley Drummle"],1,"Working together with her own half-brother Arthur."],["What is Wemmick's Castle?",["His office","His tiny house at Walworth","Jaggers's house","A real castle"],1,"Complete with a small cannon and a drawbridge."],["What does Jaggers show off at dinner?",["His paintings","The scars on his housekeeper Molly's wrists","His money","His garden"],1,"He refuses to explain how she got them."],["Who are Pip's fellow students at Mr Pocket's house?",["Wemmick and Jaggers","Bentley Drummle and Startop","Joe and Biddy","Compeyson and Arthur"],1,"One sulky and rich, the other cheerful and easy-going."]],
    tf:[["Jaggers's office is near Newgate prison.",true,"Decorated with death-masks of hanged criminals."],["Wemmick smiles constantly at the office.",false,"His mouth looks like a closed post-office slot."],["Pip lodges with a complete stranger in London.",false,"He lodges with Herbert Pocket, whom he already knew."],["Compeyson jilted Miss Havisham on her wedding morning.",true,"With help from her own half-brother Arthur."],["Wemmick behaves exactly the same at home as at the office.",false,"He keeps his Castle and his office life completely separate."],["Jaggers explains how Molly got the scars on her wrists.",false,"He refuses to explain anything about them."]],
    rw:[["Wemmick's mouth looks like a post-office slot.",0,"Right."],["Pip and Herbert had never met before London.",1,"Wrong — Pip once fought Herbert in Miss Havisham's garden."],["Herbert renames Pip Handel.",0,"Right."],["Compeyson worked alone to trick Miss Havisham.",1,"Wrong — he worked with her own half-brother Arthur."],["Bentley Drummle is a cheerful, friendly student.",1,"Wrong — he is sulky and dislikes everybody."],["Wemmick calls his old father the Aged.",0,"Right."],["Molly has scars across her wrists.",0,"Right."],["Jaggers explains exactly how old Molly is.",2,"Doesn't say — no age is given for her."]],
    halves:[["Jaggers's office stands","in a dark street near Newgate prison."],["Wemmick's mouth looks exactly like","the slot of a post-office."],["Pip's new lodgings are shared with","Herbert Pocket, the pale young gentleman from the garden."],["Herbert decides that 'Pip'","will never do, and calls him Handel instead."],["Years before, Compeyson jilted Miss Havisham","with the help of her own half-brother, Arthur."],["At his tiny Castle in Walworth,","Wemmick becomes a completely different, warmer person."],["At dinner, Jaggers shows off","the strange scars on his housekeeper's wrists."],["Pip begins to realise","that London is full of people keeping careful secrets."]],
    odd:[[["clerk","secretary","assistant","judge"],3,"A judge has a higher, different role in a court."],[["swindler","cheat","fraud","victim"],3,"A victim is the person who is tricked, not the trickster."],[["sulky","moody","cheerful","bad-tempered"],2,"Cheerful is the opposite of sulky."],[["polite","courteous","respectful","rude"],3,"Rude is the opposite of polite."],[["scar","mark","wound","smile"],3,"A smile is not a mark left by an injury."]],
    gaps:{title:"Linking words", bank:["although","because","however","so that","as soon as","in spite of"], items:[["___ Wemmick seems cold at the office, he is warm and friendly at home.","although"],["Herbert renamed Pip Handel ___ 'Pip' seemed wrong for a gentleman.","because"],["Jaggers looks frightening; ___, Wemmick trusts him completely.","however"],["Compeyson planned everything carefully ___ nobody would suspect him.","so that"],["Pip recognised Herbert ___ he saw his pale face again.","as soon as"],["Wemmick keeps his Castle separate ___ his cold, businesslike office life.","in spite of"]]},
    think:{quote:"Miss Havisham stopped every clock in the house at the exact moment she read Compeyson's letter, and has never allowed daylight into the room since.", question:"Why do you think Dickens connects Miss Havisham's stopped clocks so closely to the moment of her betrayal?", options:["Because she is interested in clocks.","Because it shows how completely one moment of pain can freeze somebody's whole life.","Because the clocks are simply broken by accident."], answer:1, note:"This detail from Unit 3 makes far more sense now that we know the true story behind it."},
    writing:[{task:"Describe Wemmick's Castle at Walworth and how different Wemmick seems there.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Describe the Castle itself","Describe the Aged Parent","Compare Wemmick at home with Wemmick at the office"],starters:["Wemmick's house is a tiny...","His old father, whom he calls...","At the office Wemmick is...","At home, however, he..."]},{task:"Imagine you are Pip at Jaggers's dinner table. Describe what you notice about Molly, and how it makes you feel.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Describe Molly and how she serves the meal","Describe the scars Jaggers shows everyone","Say what questions you want to ask, and why nobody does"],starters:["The housekeeper, Molly, moved around the table...","Jaggers suddenly took her wrist and...","I wanted to ask...","Nobody said anything because..."]}]},
  { n:7, title:"Joe's visit and Estella", unit:7,
    sum:"Joe visits Pip in London wearing his best Sunday clothes, and Pip is ashamed of him, though Joe, hurt but dignified, gently says that they no longer belong in the same places. Back home, where Orlick is now Miss Havisham's gatekeeper, Pip finds Estella grown into a beautiful young woman, and Miss Havisham urges him to love her completely. Pip admits his feelings to Herbert, who is secretly engaged to Clara himself, and later meets Estella's coach in London and takes her to Richmond, where she warns him plainly that she has no heart to give him.",
    vocab:[["EMBARRASSED","feeling uncomfortable or ashamed in front of other people"],["DIGNIFIED","behaving in a calm, serious way that deserves respect"],["GATEKEEPER","a person who controls who can enter a place through a gate"],["ADMIT","to agree, sometimes unwillingly, that something is true"],["ENGAGED","having formally agreed to marry somebody"],["ABSURD","completely ridiculous or unreasonable"],["WARN","to tell somebody about a possible danger or problem"],["HEARTLESS","without any sympathy or kindness for other people"],["COACH","a large, closed vehicle pulled by horses"],["ELEGANT","graceful and stylish in appearance or manner"],["AWKWARD","not comfortable or relaxed; embarrassing"],["BLUSH","to become red in the face because of shame or shyness"]],
    comp:[["How does Pip feel when Joe visits London?",["Delighted","Ashamed and embarrassed","Angry","Indifferent"],1,"He is ashamed of his own coldness towards Joe."],["What does Joe say before he leaves?",["That he will visit every week","That a blacksmith and a gentleman don't belong in the same places","That Pip should come home","That he is angry with Pip"],1,"He speaks with quiet dignity, not anger."],["What job does Orlick now have?",["Blacksmith","Miss Havisham's gatekeeper","Lawyer's clerk","Soldier"],1,"He now controls the very gate Pip once waited at."],["How has Estella changed?",["She is unchanged","She has grown into a beautiful, coldly perfect young woman","She has become kind","She has left Satis House"],1,"Pip's feelings for her grow more painful than ever."],["What does Miss Havisham urge Pip to do?",["Forget Estella","Love Estella completely","Marry someone else","Leave Satis House"],1,"She repeats it as though it matters desperately to her."],["What secret does Herbert admit to Pip?",["That he dislikes Pip","That he is engaged to Clara","That he is Pip's benefactor","That he knows Estella's secret"],1,"Neither family yet knows of the engagement."],["What happens at Wopsle's performance of Hamlet?",["It is a great success","The audience laughs at every serious moment","Wopsle forgets his lines completely","The play is cancelled"],1,"An absurd, embarrassing evening for poor Wopsle."],["What does Estella warn Pip on the way to Richmond?",["That she is leaving England","That she has no heart to give him","That she loves someone else","That Miss Havisham is ill"],1,"She speaks calmly, without any unkindness."]],
    tf:[["Pip is delighted when Joe comes to visit.",false,"He is ashamed of his own coldness."],["Joe says a blacksmith and a gentleman belong in the same places.",false,"He says the opposite, with quiet dignity."],["Orlick now works as Miss Havisham's gatekeeper.",true,"At the very gate of Satis House."],["Estella is unchanged since Pip last saw her.",false,"She has grown into a beautiful young woman."],["Herbert is secretly engaged to a girl called Clara.",true,"Though neither family yet knows."],["Estella tells Pip that she loves him.",false,"She warns him that she has no heart to give."]],
    rw:[["Joe arrives in London in his Sunday clothes.",0,"Right."],["Joe says he will visit Pip every week from now on.",1,"Wrong — he says he will not come again unless sent for."],["Orlick controls the gate at Satis House.",0,"Right."],["Miss Havisham tells Pip to forget Estella.",1,"Wrong — she urges him to love her completely."],["Herbert's father knows about his engagement to Clara.",2,"Doesn't say — Pip tells us neither family knows yet."],["Wopsle's Hamlet is a great theatrical success.",1,"Wrong — the audience laughs at every serious moment."],["Pip takes Estella to Richmond.",0,"Right."],["Estella warns Pip that she has no heart to give.",0,"Right."]],
    halves:[["Joe arrives in London","stiff and awkward in his best Sunday clothes."],["With quiet dignity, Joe tells Pip","that a blacksmith and a gentleman don't belong in the same places."],["Back home, Pip discovers","that Orlick is now Miss Havisham's gatekeeper."],["Estella has grown into","a beautiful, coldly perfect young woman."],["Miss Havisham urges Pip","to love Estella completely, whatever happens."],["Herbert admits to Pip","that he is secretly engaged to Clara."],["At Wopsle's absurd Hamlet,","the whole audience laughs at every tragic moment."],["On the way to Richmond,","Estella warns Pip that she has no heart to give."]],
    odd:[[["embarrassed","ashamed","awkward","proud"],3,"Proud is closer to the opposite feeling."],[["dignified","proud","respectable","clumsy"],3,"Clumsy has nothing to do with dignity."],[["absurd","ridiculous","silly","sensible"],3,"Sensible is the opposite of absurd."],[["engaged","promised","betrothed","single"],3,"Single is the opposite of engaged."],[["warn","alert","caution","reassure"],3,"Reassure means to comfort, the opposite of warn."]],
    gaps:{title:"Past continuous", bank:["was wearing","was holding","were laughing","was going","was carrying","were watching"], items:[["Joe ___ his best Sunday clothes when he arrived.","was wearing"],["He ___ his hat nervously in both hands.","was holding"],["At Wopsle's Hamlet, the audience ___ at every serious moment.","were laughing"],["Estella ___ to live with a fashionable lady in Richmond.","was going"],["Pip ___ Estella's warning inside him like a stone.","was carrying"],["Miss Havisham and Estella ___ each other closely that afternoon.","were watching"]]},
    think:{quote:"Miss Havisham whispers again and again that Pip must love Estella, whatever Estella does to him in return.", question:"What does this repeated whisper suggest about Miss Havisham's real motive?", options:["That she genuinely wants Pip and Estella to be happy together.","That she is using Pip to take revenge on men, through Estella's coldness.","That she has forgotten who Pip is."], answer:1, note:"Keep this scene in mind — Miss Havisham's true purpose for raising Estella becomes much clearer later in the story."},
    writing:[{task:"Describe Joe's visit to London and how both he and Pip feel.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Describe how Joe looks and behaves","Describe how Pip reacts to him","Say what Joe tells Pip before he leaves"],starters:["Joe arrived wearing...","At my table he seemed...","Before he left, Joe said...","I felt..."]},{task:"Estella warns Pip that she has no heart to give him. Why do you think Pip does not listen to her warning? Give your opinion.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Say exactly what Estella tells him","Say how Pip reacts","Give your own idea about why he ignores her warning"],starters:["Estella tells Pip that...","Instead of listening, Pip...","I think he ignores her because...","In my opinion..."]}]},
  { n:8, title:"The stranger in the storm", unit:8,
    sum:"Pip and Herbert run up serious debts living beyond their means, and Mrs Joe dies quietly, leaving Pip guilty about a visit to Biddy he never really keeps. At twenty-one Pip receives five hundred pounds a year and secretly arranges, through Wemmick, an anonymous partnership for Herbert with the merchant Clarriker. At Richmond, Bentley Drummle begins to court Estella, and Miss Havisham and Estella quarrel bitterly about her own coldness. Then, on a stormy night when Pip is twenty-three, a ragged, weather-worn stranger climbs the stairs: Abel Magwitch, the convict from the marshes, now transported and rich, who reveals that he, not Miss Havisham, has been Pip's benefactor all along, leaving Pip astonished and horrified.",
    vocab:[["DEBT","money that you owe to somebody else"],["FUNERAL","a ceremony held for a dead person, usually before burial"],["PARTNERSHIP","a business relationship shared between two or more people"],["ANONYMOUS","not named or identified"],["COURT","to try to win the love of somebody in order to marry them"],["STORM","very bad weather with strong wind, rain and often thunder"],["THUNDER","the loud noise that comes after lightning in a storm"],["TRANSPORTED","sent away, especially as a punishment, to a distant place"],["ASTONISHED","extremely surprised"],["PATRON","a person who gives money or support to somebody or something"],["RAGGED","wearing torn or worn-out clothes"],["HORRIFIED","feeling great shock and fear"]],
    comp:[["Why do Pip and Herbert get into debt?",["They gamble","They live beyond their means and join an expensive club","They lend money to Joe","They lose their jobs"],1,"The Finches of the Grove exist mainly to spend money."],["What does Pip feel at Mrs Joe's funeral?",["Only grief","More guilt than grief","Relief","Nothing at all"],1,"He has visited her so rarely since becoming a gentleman."],["What does Pip do with some of his new income?",["He saves it all","He secretly arranges a partnership for Herbert","He gives it to Joe","He buys a house"],1,"Through Wemmick, and completely without Herbert's knowledge."],["Who begins to court Estella at Richmond?",["Herbert","Bentley Drummle","Wemmick","Startop"],1,"He follows her everywhere, patient and rich."],["What do Miss Havisham and Estella quarrel about?",["Money","Estella's coldness","Pip","Compeyson"],1,"Estella says she is only what Miss Havisham made her."],["What happens on the stormy night when Pip is twenty-three?",["Herbert visits","A ragged stranger climbs the stairs to Pip's rooms","Joe visits","Miss Havisham dies"],1,"Thunder rolls over London as the footsteps climb."],["Who turns out to be Pip's real benefactor?",["Miss Havisham","Abel Magwitch, the convict from the marshes","Mr Jaggers","Herbert"],1,"Transported to Australia, he made a fortune and came back."],["How does Pip feel when he learns the truth?",["Delighted","Astonished and horrified","Only curious","Grateful immediately"],1,"All his hopes about Miss Havisham and Estella collapse at once."]],
    tf:[["Pip and Herbert live carefully within their income.",false,"They run up serious debts instead."],["Pip feels pure joy at Mrs Joe's funeral.",false,"He feels more guilt than grief."],["Herbert knows that Pip arranged his partnership.",false,"Pip keeps it anonymous, even from Herbert."],["Bentley Drummle begins to court Estella.",true,"Following her everywhere at Richmond."],["Miss Havisham and Estella have a warm, loving relationship.",false,"They quarrel bitterly about Estella's coldness."],["Pip's true benefactor is Miss Havisham.",false,"It turns out to be the convict Abel Magwitch."]],
    rw:[["The Finches of the Grove is an expensive dining club.",0,"Right."],["Pip visits Mrs Joe often before she dies.",1,"Wrong — he visits so rarely that he feels guilty."],["Pip receives five hundred pounds a year at twenty-one.",0,"Right."],["Herbert eventually learns who arranged his partnership.",2,"Doesn't say — Pip tells us only that Herbert never discovers it for years."],["Bentley Drummle is described as rich and sulky.",0,"Right."],["The stranger who visits Pip is about sixty years old.",0,"Right."],["The stranger has been living quietly in London for years.",1,"Wrong — he has been transported to Australia."],["Pip is delighted to learn who his benefactor really is.",1,"Wrong — he feels horror, not delight."]],
    halves:[["Pip and Herbert run up debts","living far beyond what they can really afford."],["At Mrs Joe's funeral,","Pip feels more guilt than grief."],["Through Wemmick, Pip secretly arranges","an anonymous partnership for Herbert."],["At Richmond, Bentley Drummle","begins to court Estella patiently."],["Miss Havisham and Estella quarrel","bitterly about Estella's own coldness."],["On a stormy night,","a ragged stranger climbs the stairs to Pip's rooms."],["The stranger turns out to be","Abel Magwitch, the convict from the marshes."],["Pip realises with horror","that his benefactor was never Miss Havisham at all."]],
    odd:[[["debt","loan","credit owed","savings"],3,"Savings is money you have, not money you owe."],[["astonished","amazed","shocked","expected"],3,"Expected is the opposite of astonished."],[["ragged","torn","shabby","smart"],3,"Smart is the opposite of ragged."],[["horrified","terrified","disgusted","pleased"],3,"Pleased is the opposite of horrified."],[["anonymous","unnamed","unknown","famous"],3,"Famous is the opposite of anonymous."]],
    gaps:{title:"Comparatives", bank:["more guilty","richer","louder","more ragged","colder","stranger"], items:[["Pip felt ___ than sad at his sister's funeral.","more guilty"],["Magwitch had become much ___ than the starving convict on the marsh.","richer"],["The thunder grew ___ as the footsteps climbed the stairs.","louder"],["The visitor looked even ___ than Pip remembered from years before.","more ragged"],["That stormy night felt ___ than any winter Pip had known in London.","colder"],["Nothing in Pip's whole life had ever felt ___ than that moment.","stranger"]]},
    think:{quote:"Pip stares at the ragged, weather-worn man in his doorway and realises, all at once, that this convict from the marshes, not Miss Havisham, is his secret benefactor.", question:"Why is this moment such an important turning point in the story?", options:["Because Pip finally becomes rich.","Because everything Pip believed about his future, Estella and his own worth is suddenly proved false.","Because Magwitch asks Pip for money."], answer:1, note:"This single stormy night undoes almost everything Pip has assumed since Chapter 5 — watch how his whole idea of being a gentleman changes from here on."},
    writing:[{task:"Describe the stormy night when the stranger climbed the stairs to Pip's rooms.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Describe the storm outside","Describe the stranger's appearance","Say what he tells Pip"],starters:["Thunder rolled over London as...","In the doorway stood...","He told me that...","I felt..."]},{task:"Why do you think Pip feels horror instead of gratitude when he learns Magwitch is his benefactor? Give your opinion.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Say what Pip had believed before this night","Say what he learns instead","Give your own idea about his feelings"],starters:["Before this night, Pip had believed that...","Instead, he learns that...","I think he feels horror because...","In my opinion..."]}]},
  { n:9, title:"Magwitch's story", unit:9,
    sum:"Pip hides Magwitch, now using the alias Provis, in lodgings on Essex Street, since returning to England means death for him. Herbert and Pip hear the whole story of his childhood poverty, his early prison sentences, and his partnership with the gentleman swindler Compeyson, who received an unfairly lighter sentence at their joint trial and turns out to be the very man who once jilted Miss Havisham. Pip decides he can no longer take Magwitch's money and resolves instead to help him escape from England at great risk to them both.",
    vocab:[["HIDE","to put or keep somebody or something where they cannot be seen"],["RISK","the possibility of danger, loss or injury"],["POVERTY","the state of being extremely poor"],["SENTENCE","the punishment given by a court to somebody found guilty"],["TRIAL","a formal examination of evidence in a court of law"],["UNFAIR","not treating people equally or justly"],["ABANDON","to leave somebody or something, especially in a difficult situation"],["BOAST","to talk with too much pride about your achievements"],["RESCUE","to save somebody from danger"],["ALIAS","a false or different name used to hide your real identity"],["GUILT","a feeling of having done something wrong"],["DANGEROUS","likely to cause harm or injury"]],
    comp:[["Why must Pip hide Magwitch in London?",["He is embarrassed of him","Returning from transportation means death for Magwitch if caught","He owes money","He wants to keep the fortune secret"],1,"Returning is a crime punishable by death."],["What alias does Magwitch use?",["Mr Handel","Mr Provis","Mr Pocket","Mr Wemmick"],1,"A safe, ordinary name to hide behind."],["What was Magwitch's childhood like?",["Rich and happy","Poor, moving between prisons and workhouses","Spent at sea","Spent in France"],1,"Nobody was ever kind to him without wanting something back."],["Who was Compeyson?",["A lawyer","A gentleman swindler who worked with Magwitch","A soldier","Miss Havisham's brother"],1,"He used Magwitch's rough hands for the dangerous work."],["What happened at their joint trial?",["Both were freed","Compeyson received an unfairly lighter sentence","Magwitch was freed","Both received the same sentence"],1,"His gentleman's manners impressed the court."],["What surprising connection do Pip and Herbert discover?",["Compeyson is Magwitch's brother","Compeyson is the same man who jilted Miss Havisham","Compeyson is Wemmick's relative","Compeyson is dead"],1,"Their two separate stories turn out to be connected."],["What does Pip overhear at the Blue Boar?",["News of Magwitch","Drummle boasting about winning Estella","Joe's arrival","Miss Havisham's illness"],1,"He feels sick with jealousy he cannot show."],["What do Pip and Herbert decide to do?",["Report Magwitch to the police","Help Magwitch escape from England","Ask Miss Havisham for help","Do nothing"],1,"At great risk to themselves as well as to him."]],
    tf:[["Returning to England is safe for Magwitch.",false,"It is a crime punishable by death."],["Magwitch uses the alias Mr Provis.",true,"A safe, ordinary name."],["Magwitch had a happy, comfortable childhood.",false,"He grew up in complete poverty."],["Compeyson received a lighter sentence than Magwitch.",true,"Because of his gentleman's manners and expensive lawyer."],["Compeyson is a stranger to Miss Havisham's story.",false,"He is the very man who jilted her."],["Pip and Herbert decide to help Magwitch escape England.",true,"To get him safely away before Compeyson finds him."]],
    rw:[["Pip and Herbert find Magwitch lodgings on Essex Street.",0,"Right."],["Magwitch grew up with a large, loving family.",1,"Wrong — he grew up in poverty with no family he could remember."],["Compeyson dressed and spoke like a gentleman.",0,"Right — while Magwitch did the dangerous work."],["Magwitch received a lighter sentence than Compeyson.",1,"Wrong — Compeyson's was lighter."],["Compeyson is the man who jilted Miss Havisham.",0,"Right."],["Drummle boasts about Estella at the Blue Boar.",0,"Right."],["Pip decides to keep taking Magwitch's money.",1,"Wrong — he decides he cannot honestly take more."],["Compeyson is currently in prison.",2,"Doesn't say — Pip fears he is somewhere nearby, but we are not told exactly where."]],
    halves:[["Pip hides Magwitch in London","under the safe alias of Mr Provis."],["Returning from transportation","is a crime punishable by death."],["Magwitch grew up","in complete poverty, moving between prisons and workhouses."],["Compeyson used Magwitch's rough hands","to do the dangerous, dirty work of their swindles."],["At their joint trial,","Compeyson received an unfairly lighter sentence."],["Pip and Herbert realise","that Compeyson once jilted Miss Havisham too."],["At the Blue Boar,","Pip overhears Drummle boasting about Estella."],["Pip decides he can no longer","take Magwitch's dangerous, hard-earned money."]],
    odd:[[["poverty","hardship","wealth","need"],2,"Wealth is the opposite of poverty."],[["sentence","punishment","penalty","reward"],3,"A reward is the opposite of a punishment."],[["unfair","unjust","biased","fair"],3,"Fair is the opposite of unfair."],[["boast","brag","show off","apologise"],3,"To apologise is the opposite of boasting."],[["rescue","save","free","abandon"],3,"To abandon is the opposite of to rescue."]],
    gaps:{title:"Reported speech", bank:["had grown","was","had led","could not","would help","had jilted"], items:[["Magwitch told them that he ___ up in complete poverty.","had grown"],["He said that Compeyson ___ the cleverer of the two.","was"],["He insisted that Compeyson ___ every one of their swindles.","had led"],["Pip realised that he ___ take any more of the money.","could not"],["Herbert promised that he ___ Pip get Magwitch away safely.","would help"],["Pip understood that Compeyson ___ Miss Havisham years before.","had jilted"]]},
    think:{quote:"Compeyson dressed well and spoke well, and let Magwitch's rough hands do all the dangerous work, before walking away from their trial with the lighter sentence.", question:"What is Dickens suggesting about the difference between Magwitch and Compeyson?", options:["That Magwitch is simply a worse criminal than Compeyson.","That looking and sounding respectable can protect a truly guilty man more than honesty protects an unlucky one.","That the law always punishes the right person."], answer:1, note:"This idea — that appearances can matter more than the truth — runs through the whole novel, from Pip's shame about his hands to Compeyson's escape from justice."},
    writing:[{task:"Describe the evening Magwitch tells Pip and Herbert the story of his life.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Describe where and how he tells his story","Describe his childhood","Describe his partnership with Compeyson"],starters:["That evening, close by the fire, Magwitch began...","As a boy, he...","Compeyson was a man who...","At their trial..."]},{task:"Why does Pip decide he can no longer take Magwitch's money? Give your opinion, using details from the unit.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Say what taking the money would mean now","Say what Pip decides to do instead","Give your own idea about his change of heart"],starters:["Now that Pip knows the danger Magwitch is in,...","He decides that...","Instead, he and Herbert plan to...","I think this shows that Pip..."]}]},
  { n:10, title:"Don't go home", unit:10,
    sum:"Pip accuses Miss Havisham of using him cruelly and asks her to finish paying for Herbert's partnership; Estella coldly announces that she will marry Drummle, and Pip pours out his love for her in vain. A warning note from Wemmick tells Pip not to go home, since Compeyson is somewhere in London, and Magwitch is moved downstream to Clara's house at Mill Pond Bank while Herbert and Pip practise rowing to prepare his escape on a foreign steamer. At the theatre, Wopsle tells Pip that Compeyson sat right behind him, and at a dinner with Jaggers, Pip finally realises, from her eyes and her hands, that the housekeeper Molly is Estella's real mother.",
    vocab:[["ACCUSE","to say that somebody has done something wrong"],["FORGIVE","to stop being angry with somebody for something they did"],["ANNOUNCE","to tell people something officially or publicly"],["REJECT","to refuse to accept somebody or something"],["POUR","to make a liquid, or your feelings, flow out"],["ROW","to move a boat through water using oars"],["DOWNSTREAM","in the direction that a river flows"],["DISGUISE","to change your appearance so that people cannot recognise you"],["WARNING","something said to tell somebody about a possible danger"],["REALISE","to become aware of a fact for the first time"],["THEATRE","a building where plays are performed"],["RESEMBLANCE","a similarity in appearance between two people or things"]],
    comp:[["What does Pip accuse Miss Havisham of?",["Stealing money","Using him unfairly for years","Lying to Estella","Nothing"],1,"He feels she let him believe things that were never true."],["What does Pip ask Miss Havisham to do?",["Give him more money","Finish paying for Herbert's partnership","Forgive Estella","Leave Satis House"],1,"A request only for Herbert's sake, not his own."],["What does Estella announce?",["That she loves Pip","That she will marry Bentley Drummle","That she is leaving England","That she forgives Miss Havisham"],1,"Said without the smallest warmth in her voice."],["What warning does Wemmick send Pip?",["Come home at once","Don't go home","Leave London forever","Visit Jaggers"],1,"Compeyson is somewhere in the city, watching."],["Where is Magwitch moved to?",["Satis House","Clara's house at Mill Pond Bank","Newgate prison","Wemmick's Castle"],1,"A quiet spot for a future escape by river."],["Why do Pip and Herbert practise rowing?",["For fun","To prepare Magwitch's escape by river","To visit Richmond","To meet Wopsle"],1,"So their boat becomes a familiar, harmless sight."],["What does Wopsle tell Pip at the theatre?",["That Estella is there","That Compeyson sat directly behind him","That Joe is ill","That Magwitch is caught"],1,"A man Wopsle recognises from Magwitch's old trial."],["What does Pip realise about Molly?",["That she is Miss Havisham's sister","That she is Estella's real mother","That she is Compeyson's wife","That she is innocent"],1,"Her eyes and hands resemble Estella's exactly."]],
    tf:[["Pip tells Miss Havisham she has treated him fairly.",false,"He tells her she has used him unfairly."],["Estella announces she will marry Bentley Drummle.",true,"Without the smallest warmth in her voice."],["Wemmick's note tells Pip to come home immediately.",false,"It warns him: Don't go home."],["Magwitch is moved to a house by the river.",true,"At Clara's house on Mill Pond Bank."],["Pip and Herbert practise rowing to prepare an escape.",true,"So their boat looks like a familiar, harmless sight."],["Pip discovers that Molly is a stranger to Estella.",false,"He realises she is Estella's real mother."]],
    rw:[["Pip asks Miss Havisham to finish paying for Herbert's partnership.",0,"Right."],["Estella tells Pip she loves him.",1,"Wrong — she announces she will marry Drummle instead."],["Estella says she rejects Pip out of cruelty.",1,"Wrong — she says there is simply nothing in her to give."],["Wemmick's warning note says Compeyson is in London.",0,"Right."],["Magwitch is moved downstream to Mill Pond Bank.",0,"Right."],["Wopsle recognises the man sitting behind Pip at the theatre.",0,"Right — from Magwitch's old trial."],["Pip recognises Compeyson's face immediately when Wopsle points him out.",2,"Doesn't say — Pip only tells us Wopsle recognised the man."],["Molly's eyes and hands remind Pip of Estella.",0,"Right."]],
    halves:[["Pip tells Miss Havisham","that she has used him unfairly for years."],["He asks her only","to finish paying for Herbert's partnership."],["Estella coldly announces","that she has decided to marry Bentley Drummle."],["Pip pours out his love,","but Estella says there is nothing in her to give."],["Wemmick's note warns Pip","with three frightening words: Don't go home."],["Magwitch is moved downstream","to Clara's quiet house at Mill Pond Bank."],["At the theatre, Wopsle tells Pip","that Compeyson has been sitting right behind him."],["Studying Molly's eyes and hands,","Pip realises she is Estella's real mother."]],
    odd:[[["accuse","blame","charge","praise"],3,"Praise is the opposite of accusing."],[["reject","refuse","turn down","accept"],3,"Accept is the opposite of reject."],[["forgive","pardon","excuse","punish"],3,"Punish is the opposite of forgive."],[["warning","caution","alert","reassurance"],3,"Reassurance calms fear, a warning creates it."],[["resemblance","similarity","likeness","difference"],3,"Difference is the opposite of resemblance."]],
    gaps:{title:"Reported speech", bank:["would marry","had used","could not","had been watching","was sitting","had realised"], items:[["Estella announced that she ___ Bentley Drummle.","would marry"],["Pip told Miss Havisham that she ___ him unfairly.","had used"],["Estella said that she ___ give Pip what he wanted.","could not"],["Wemmick warned Pip that somebody ___ his rooms.","had been watching"],["Wopsle said that a strange man ___ right behind Pip.","was sitting"],["Pip told Herbert that he ___ who Molly really was.","had realised"]]},
    think:{quote:"Studying Molly's quiet face and her strong hands as she pours the wine, Pip slowly recognises Estella's own eyes looking back at him.", question:"Why does Dickens delay this discovery until now, so long after we first meet Molly?", options:["Because it is not an important detail.","Because letting readers half-forget her makes the sudden recognition feel as shocking to us as it is to Pip.","Because Molly's identity was always obvious."], answer:1, note:"Notice how many small details from earlier units — Molly's scarred wrists, Estella's coldness, Magwitch's past — are quietly connecting into one picture."},
    writing:[{task:"Describe the moment Estella tells Pip she will marry Bentley Drummle.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Describe how Estella speaks and behaves","Describe what Pip says to her","Say how Pip feels afterwards"],starters:["Estella announced, without any warmth, that...","I told her that I had loved her since...","She answered that...","I felt..."]},{task:"Describe the moment Pip realises that Molly is Estella's mother.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Describe what Pip notices about Molly","Say what this reminds him of","Say how this discovery makes him feel"],starters:["At dinner, Pip found himself studying...","Her eyes and her hands reminded him of...","Slowly he realised that...","This discovery made him feel..."]}]},
  { n:11, title:"Fire and the lime-kiln", unit:11,
    sum:"Full of remorse, Miss Havisham gives Pip the money to finish Herbert's partnership and begs his forgiveness on her knees; moments later her old wedding dress catches fire, and Pip burns his hands badly saving her life. Herbert then reveals that Magwitch once had a wife and a baby daughter thought lost forever, and Mr Jaggers finally admits that he himself placed that same little girl with Miss Havisham years ago: Estella is Magwitch's own daughter. Soon afterwards Orlick lures Pip to a lonely sluice-house by the lime-kiln, ties him up, confesses that he attacked Mrs Joe, and is about to kill him when Herbert, Startop and Trabb's boy burst in just in time.",
    vocab:[["REMORSE","a strong feeling of sadness and regret about something you have done"],["FORGIVENESS","the act of forgiving somebody"],["FLAME","the hot, bright, burning part of a fire"],["BURN","to be damaged or destroyed by fire, or to injure with fire or heat"],["LURE","to persuade somebody to go somewhere by promising them something"],["TRAP","a trick that is intended to catch or deceive somebody"],["REVEAL","to make known something that was secret or hidden"],["TIE","to fasten somebody or something with rope or string"],["SLUICE","a gate that controls the flow of water"],["RESCUE","to save somebody from danger"],["DESPERATE","feeling that you will do anything to change a bad situation"],["LOYAL","firm and faithful in your support of somebody"]],
    comp:[["What does Miss Havisham give Pip, full of remorse?",["A letter","The money to finish Herbert's partnership","A ring","Nothing"],1,"She also begs his forgiveness on her knees."],["What happens to Miss Havisham's dress?",["It is torn","It catches fire from the fireplace","It is stolen","Nothing happens"],1,"A sudden flame catches its ancient, yellowed fabric."],["How is Pip hurt?",["He is not hurt","He burns his hands and arms saving her","He breaks his leg","He is poisoned"],1,"He smothers the flames with a tablecloth and his own coat."],["What does Herbert tell Pip about Magwitch's past?",["Nothing new","That Magwitch once had a wife and a lost daughter","That Magwitch is innocent","That Magwitch is rich"],1,"A detail that leads to an extraordinary discovery."],["What does Jaggers finally admit?",["That he is Pip's benefactor","That he placed Magwitch's daughter with Miss Havisham","That Compeyson is dead","That Estella is rich"],1,"He never says it plainly, but Pip understands completely."],["Who turns out to be Estella's real father?",["Mr Jaggers","Abel Magwitch","Compeyson","Joe"],1,"The convict Pip once helped on the marshes."],["Who lures Pip to the sluice-house?",["Compeyson","Orlick","Drummle","Wemmick"],1,"With an unsigned note about Provis."],["Who rescues Pip at the sluice-house?",["Joe alone","Herbert, Startop and Trabb's boy","Miss Havisham","Jaggers"],1,"They arrive in the very last possible moment."]],
    tf:[["Miss Havisham feels no remorse for what she has done.",false,"She begs Pip's forgiveness on her knees."],["Pip is badly burned trying to save Miss Havisham.",true,"His hands and arms are badly hurt."],["Herbert tells Pip that Magwitch never had any family.",false,"He tells Pip about a wife and a lost daughter."],["Jaggers admits he placed Magwitch's daughter with Miss Havisham.",true,"Though he never says it in so many plain words."],["Orlick lures Pip to the sluice-house with a friendly invitation.",false,"He uses an unsigned, deceptive note."],["Nobody rescues Pip at the sluice-house.",false,"Herbert, Startop and Trabb's boy arrive just in time."]],
    rw:[["Miss Havisham gives Pip money for Herbert's partnership.",0,"Right."],["Miss Havisham's dress catches fire from a candle she is holding.",2,"Doesn't say — Pip only tells us a flame catches it from the fireplace."],["Pip saves Miss Havisham using a tablecloth and his coat.",0,"Right."],["Magwitch never had a wife or children.",1,"Wrong — Herbert reveals he had a wife and a daughter."],["Estella is revealed to be Magwitch's daughter.",0,"Right."],["Orlick admits he attacked Mrs Joe.",0,"Right."],["Orlick is caught and arrested at the sluice-house.",1,"Wrong — he escapes into the darkness of the marsh."],["Herbert, Startop and Trabb's boy save Pip's life.",0,"Right."]],
    halves:[["Full of remorse, Miss Havisham","begs Pip's forgiveness on her knees."],["A sudden flame catches","the edge of her old wedding dress."],["Pip burns his hands badly","smothering the flames with a tablecloth and his coat."],["Herbert reveals that Magwitch","once had a wife and a lost baby daughter."],["Jaggers finally admits","that he placed that same girl with Miss Havisham."],["Pip realises with astonishment","that Estella is Magwitch's own daughter."],["Orlick lures Pip","to a lonely sluice-house with a false note."],["Herbert, Startop and Trabb's boy","burst in just before Orlick can kill Pip."]],
    odd:[[["remorse","regret","guilt","pride"],3,"Pride is not a feeling of sorrow for wrongdoing."],[["flame","fire","blaze","ash"],3,"Ash is what remains after a fire, not the fire itself."],[["lure","tempt","attract","warn"],3,"To warn is the opposite of luring somebody into danger."],[["reveal","disclose","uncover","hide"],3,"To hide is the opposite of to reveal."],[["desperate","hopeless","frantic","calm"],3,"Calm is the opposite of desperate."]],
    gaps:{title:"Present perfect", bank:["has given","has caught fire","have revealed","has admitted","have arrived","has escaped"], items:[["Miss Havisham ___ Pip the money for Herbert at last.","has given"],["Her old wedding dress ___ from the fireplace.","has caught fire"],["Herbert's words ___ an extraordinary secret about Magwitch.","have revealed"],["Jaggers finally ___ where the little girl was placed.","has admitted"],["Herbert, Startop and Trabb's boy ___ just in time.","have arrived"],["Orlick ___ into the darkness of the marsh.","has escaped"]]},
    think:{quote:"Jaggers never says the plain words, but Pip understands completely: Estella, cold and beautiful, is the daughter of the ragged convict who calls himself Pip's second father.", question:"Why might Dickens connect Pip's two most important relationships — with Magwitch and with Estella — in this way?", options:["It is simply a coincidence with no deeper meaning.","It shows that the gentleman's world Pip has admired and the criminal's world he has feared are, in truth, the very same world.","It proves that Estella has always known the truth."], answer:1, note:"This is one of the novel's central ideas: the rich and the poor, the respectable and the criminal, are far more closely connected than Pip — or the reader — first believed."},
    writing:[{task:"Describe the moment Miss Havisham's dress catches fire and how Pip saves her.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Describe what happens to the dress","Describe what Pip does to save her","Say how Pip is hurt"],starters:["Suddenly a flame...","Pip threw himself at her and...","He used...","Afterwards, his hands..."]},{task:"Describe what happens when Pip is trapped by Orlick in the sluice-house.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Say how Orlick lures Pip there","Describe what Orlick confesses","Say how Pip is finally saved"],starters:["An unsigned note asked Pip to...","Inside, Orlick confessed that...","Just as Orlick was about to...","Herbert, Startop and Trabb's boy..."]}]},
  { n:12, title:"The river and the end", unit:12,
    sum:"The escape down the Thames fails when a police galley carrying Compeyson intercepts the boat; the two old enemies struggle in the water, Compeyson drowns, and Magwitch is badly hurt and captured, his whole fortune going to the Crown. Pip visits him daily in the prison hospital and is with him when he dies, comforted by the truth that his daughter is alive and loved. Pip himself falls seriously ill, and Joe nurses him back to health, quietly pays his debts and slips away; when Pip returns to propose to Biddy, he finds it is her wedding day to Joe. After eleven years working abroad with Herbert, Pip returns to England and meets Estella, widowed and softened, in the ruined garden of Satis House, and they leave together, hand in hand.",
    vocab:[["DROWN","to die under water because you cannot breathe"],["CAPTURE","to catch a person and take control of them"],["CROWN","the state, especially as represented by a king or queen"],["HOSPITAL","a place where sick or injured people are treated"],["WIDOWED","having had your husband or wife die, and not remarried"],["SOFTENED","made gentler or less hard in feeling or behaviour"],["RUINED","badly damaged, especially through age or neglect"],["GALLEY","a large rowing boat, especially one used by police or the navy"],["INTERCEPT","to stop somebody or something before they reach their destination"],["STRUGGLE","to fight or make a great physical effort"],["ILL","not in good health; sick"],["ABROAD","in or to a foreign country"]],
    comp:[["What happens when the police galley appears?",["It lets them pass","It moves to intercept their boat, with Compeyson aboard","It sinks","It escorts them safely"],1,"Compeyson stands in the bow, pointing at Magwitch."],["What happens to Compeyson?",["He escapes","He drowns after struggling with Magwitch in the water","He is arrested calmly","He rescues Magwitch"],1,"He is never properly recovered from the river."],["What happens to Magwitch's fortune?",["Pip keeps it","It goes to the Crown","Herbert inherits it","It is given to Estella"],1,"A convict's property automatically belongs to the state."],["Where does Pip visit Magwitch every day?",["At Satis House","In the prison hospital","At Jaggers's office","At the forge"],1,"Sitting quietly by his bed as his health worsens."],["What does Pip tell Magwitch before he dies?",["That his fortune is safe","That his daughter is alive and loved","That Compeyson survived","Nothing important"],1,"A kind of peace comes over his tired face."],["Who nurses Pip through his own illness?",["Biddy","Joe","Herbert","Wemmick"],1,"He also quietly pays off Pip's remaining debts."],["What does Pip discover when he goes to propose to Biddy?",["She has left the village","It is her wedding day, to Joe","She has died","She refuses to see him"],1,"A gentle, fitting surprise after everything Pip has been through."],["Where does Pip finally meet Estella again?",["In London","In the ruined garden of Satis House","At the forge","In Australia"],1,"Both of them changed, softened by everything that has happened."]],
    tf:[["The police galley lets Magwitch's boat pass freely.",false,"It moves to intercept them, with Compeyson aboard."],["Compeyson drowns in the struggle with Magwitch.",true,"He is never properly recovered from the river."],["Magwitch is allowed to keep his fortune.",false,"It goes automatically to the Crown."],["Pip is with Magwitch when he dies.",true,"He tells him his daughter is alive and loved."],["Pip discovers that Biddy is marrying Joe.",true,"On the very morning he comes to propose to her himself."],["Pip and Estella meet again in London.",false,"They meet in the ruined garden of Satis House."]],
    rw:[["Compeyson stands in the bow of the police galley.",0,"Right."],["Magwitch and Compeyson struggle in the water.",0,"Right."],["Magwitch is only slightly hurt when he is captured.",1,"Wrong — he is badly hurt, with a wound and a broken rib."],["Magwitch's fortune passes to Pip.",1,"Wrong — it goes to the Crown."],["Pip visits Magwitch every day in the prison hospital.",0,"Right."],["Joe pays off Pip's debts without mentioning it.",0,"Right."],["Pip arrives at the forge on Biddy's wedding day.",0,"Right."],["Herbert is happily married to Clara by the time Pip returns.",2,"Doesn't say — we know he was engaged to her earlier, but the ending does not mention their marriage."]],
    halves:[["A police galley appears","and moves to intercept Magwitch's boat, with Compeyson aboard."],["Magwitch and Compeyson struggle","together beneath the surface of the river."],["Compeyson drowns,","and Magwitch is pulled from the water badly hurt."],["Because he is a convict,","Magwitch's whole fortune passes automatically to the Crown."],["Pip visits Magwitch every day","in the prison hospital until he dies."],["Joe nurses Pip through his illness","and quietly pays off all his debts."],["Pip arrives at the forge","on the very morning of Biddy and Joe's wedding."],["After eleven years abroad,","Pip meets Estella again in the ruined garden of Satis House."]],
    odd:[[["drown","sink","survive","suffocate"],2,"Survive is the opposite of drowning."],[["capture","catch","seize","release"],3,"Release is the opposite of capture."],[["ruined","decayed","broken-down","perfect"],3,"Perfect is the opposite of ruined."],[["struggle","fight","wrestle","surrender"],3,"Surrender means to stop fighting."],[["ill","sick","unwell","healthy"],3,"Healthy is the opposite of ill."]],
    gaps:{title:"Linking words", bank:["although","because","however","so that","before","in spite of"], items:[["___ Magwitch is badly hurt, he is still arrested and put on trial.","although"],["He struggled with Compeyson ___ he had waited years for this moment.","because"],["His fortune goes to the Crown; ___, Pip does not care about the money any more.","however"],["Pip visited him daily ___ he would not die alone.","so that"],["Joe slipped quietly away ___ Pip could properly thank him.","before"],["Estella took Pip's hand ___ everything that had happened between them.","in spite of"]]},
    think:{quote:"In the ruined garden of Satis House, where Pip once cried behind a wall about his coarse hands and thick boots, he holds out his hand to Estella one last time, and this time she takes it.", question:"Why does Dickens choose to end the story back in this same garden where it all began?", options:["Because Pip has nowhere else left to go.","Because it shows how completely both Pip and Estella have changed since that first, painful afternoon.","Because Miss Havisham asked them to meet there."], answer:1, note:"From the churchyard in Unit 1 to this ruined garden, Pip's whole story has been about learning what really matters — not being uncommon, but being loyal, honest and, in the end, forgiving."},
    writing:[{task:"Describe the struggle in the river between Magwitch and Compeyson, and what happens afterwards.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Describe the moment the police galley appears","Describe the struggle in the water","Say what happens to each man afterwards"],starters:["Suddenly a police galley...","Magwitch threw himself at...","In the water, they...","Afterwards, Compeyson... while Magwitch..."]},{task:"How has Pip changed by the end of the story? Give your opinion, using examples from the whole book.",target:"60-90 words",tips:["Say how Pip felt about Joe and the forge at the beginning","Say how he treats Joe, Biddy and Magwitch by the end","Give your own opinion about what he has learned"],starters:["At the beginning of the story, Pip felt...","By the end, however, he...","With Magwitch, he shows that he has learned...","In my opinion, the most important change is..."]}]}
