const READINGS = {
1:[
"My name is Philip Pirrip, but everyone has always called me Pip. I never saw my father or my mother, or any picture of them, so my first ideas about them came from the shapes of the letters on their gravestones in our marsh churchyard.",
"I was an orphan, and I lived with my sister, whom everyone called Mrs Joe, and her husband Joe Gargery, the village blacksmith. Mrs Joe often reminded me that she had brought me up by hand, and her hand was usually a hard one. Joe was a big, gentle man with pale blue eyes, and he was the only real friend of my childhood.",
"On a cold, grey Christmas Eve I went alone to the churchyard to look at the gravestones of my parents and my five little brothers, who had all died as babies. The marsh stretched out flat and dark all around me, with the grey river beyond it and the sea somewhere further still.",
"Suddenly a rough voice growled, “Stop that crying, and stay exactly where you are!” from among the graves, and a man rose up in front of me. He had no hat, broken shoes, and an old rag tied round his head. There was an iron on one leg, and his clothes were torn and soaked. He looked like a man who had been living in ditches and beaten by brambles and stones.",
"He seized me by the chin and turned me upside down, so that the church seemed to jump over its own weathercock. Then he set me on a gravestone and stood over me, terrified himself, I thought, though I was far more terrified than he was.",
"“Listen carefully,” he said, in a low and threatening whisper, “you bring me a file, and you bring me food. Fail me, and I will find you wherever you hide, and you will be sorry you were ever born.”",
"I promised, and felt a shiver of fear from head to foot. He asked where I lived, and I pointed across the marsh towards the forge. Then he let me go, and I ran home without once looking back, expecting his hand on my shoulder at every step.",
"At home, Joe was alone by the fire; Mrs Joe had gone out. He told me that convicts had escaped from the prison ships in the river that very afternoon, and that soldiers with guns were searching the marsh for them. I said nothing about the man among the graves, though the secret felt like a stone in my chest.",
"I could not sleep that night. I kept thinking of the file in Joe's forge and the last piece of pie in the pantry, and of the man's threatening whisper. Long before it was light, I got up and crept downstairs like a small, guilty thief in my own house.",
"I took bread, cheese, a whole pork pie, a bottle of brandy, and, from the dark forge, one of Joe's files. Then I let myself out into a white, freezing mist and ran towards the marsh, sure that every gate and every cow was calling out, “A convict! Stop him!”",
"On the marsh I met a second man, sitting with his face turned away, dressed almost exactly like my own convict, with the same kind of iron on his leg. For one dreadful moment I thought I had found the wrong prisoner, but he pushed me aside without a word and limped off into the mist.",
"My own man was waiting near the old battery, shaking with cold, his eyes hungry and wild. He tore at the pie and the bread and the cheese like a hungry dog, hardly stopping to breathe, and drank the brandy in one long swallow.",
"“Was there a young man with you?” he asked suddenly, looking round in fear. I described the other convict I had seen, and my man grew wilder still, muttering that he would hunt him down like an animal on that lonely marsh. Then, without another word, he turned back to filing at the iron on his leg, and I left him to it and ran home through the mist as fast as my legs would carry me."
],
2:[
"Christmas dinner that year was at our house, with our neighbours Mr Wopsle, Mr Hubble and his wife, and my Uncle Pumblechook, a large, pompous corn-merchant who always spoke to me as though I were a very small and very stupid animal.",
"I sat at the table in absolute agony, certain that at any moment somebody would ask for the pork pie and discover that it, and a good deal of brandy, had disappeared in the night.",
"Uncle Pumblechook praised the pork, then the brandy, not knowing that I had watered his glass to hide what I had taken from the bottle. I felt more guilty with every kind word they said about food I had stolen for a starving stranger.",
"Then, just as Mrs Joe rose to fetch the pie, the door burst open and a sergeant walked in with a party of soldiers, their muskets shining in the candlelight. “A pair of handcuffs here want a link or two mended,” he said, holding them out to Joe.",
"I nearly fainted with relief and terror together, for they had not come for the pie at all. The sergeant explained that two convicts had escaped from the prison ships, and that soldiers were needed at once to hunt them down across the marsh.",
"Joe mended the handcuffs quickly at his forge, and then, to my great surprise, he asked to join the hunt himself. I begged so hard to go too that Mrs Joe, eager to be rid of me, let me follow, wrapped up against the cold.",
"We crossed the dark marsh with the soldiers, our lanterns making small circles of light in the freezing mist. Somewhere ahead we heard shouting, and then, suddenly, we came upon a deep ditch where two men were rolling over each other like fighting dogs, covered in mud and blood.",
"The soldiers pulled the men apart. One was my convict; the other was the stranger I had met alone on the marsh. Each accused the other of trying to commit murder, and each looked as wild and hopeless as an animal caught in a trap.",
"As the soldiers led them away, my convict suddenly confessed, in front of everyone, that he had broken into the blacksmith's pantry that morning and stolen the food himself. He never once looked at me, but I understood, with a shock, that he was protecting me.",
"Joe spoke up quietly. “You were welcome to every bit of it, friend, whatever trouble you're in,” he said. “A hungry man's a hungry man, and that's all there is to it.” It was a generous thing to say to a man in chains, and nobody on that cold, muddy marsh expected it.",
"The convict looked almost grateful, though he said nothing more before the soldiers marched both men down to a waiting boat.",
"So ended the great capture, and so ended, I thought, any hope the two men had of a real escape. I watched the boat carry them back towards the black hulk of the prison ship, and for the first time in that whole terrible business, I felt sorry rather than afraid."
],
3:[
"A few weeks later my uncle Pumblechook came to the forge with wonderful news: a rich, strange lady named Miss Havisham, who lived alone at Satis House, wanted a boy to come and play there. Mrs Joe scrubbed me until I shone and sent me off with my uncle, warning me to be forever grateful.",
"Satis House was a gloomy old mansion of dark brick, with a courtyard of weeds and a gate that nobody used any more. A strange, serious girl about my own age let me in and led me up a dark staircase without a single candle to light our way.",
"In an upstairs room every clock had stopped at twenty minutes to nine, and the curtains were kept shut so that no daylight ever entered. There, in a chair, sat the strangest lady I have ever seen: dressed in a wedding gown that had once been white and was now the colour of an old, yellow tooth.",
"One shoe was on and one shoe was off; her veil was half arranged; and on the table beside her stood a wedding-cake, black with age, rotten and covered in cobwebs, that mice had eaten into holes over the years. Miss Havisham told me she had not seen the sun since before I was born.",
"She told me to call the girl, whose name was Estella, and to play cards with her. Estella looked at me with such open dislike that I felt my face go hot before we had even begun.",
"Estella laughed a short, cold laugh every time I used the wrong word for a card, and looked me up and down as though I were something unpleasant she had found in the yard. “Rough hands, heavy boots — he's more suited to a forge than a card table,” she said to Miss Havisham, not even bothering to lower her voice.",
"I had never thought about my hands or my boots before that afternoon, but from that moment I saw them as she saw them: the hands and boots of a common labouring boy, fit only for a blacksmith's forge.",
"Miss Havisham watched us both with a strange, hungry pleasure, and told Estella to break my heart when she grew up, and to break it well. I did not understand her then, though the words stayed with me for years afterwards.",
"By the time I left, I had never once been ashamed of my home until Estella began to mock me, and taught me, with one cold little laugh, to be ashamed of it. I cried behind the brewery wall before Pumblechook could see me.",
"When my uncle asked how I had got on, pride would not let me tell the truth, so I invented a wild story: that Miss Havisham sat in a black velvet coach, that we had eaten cake and wine off gold plates, and that four enormous dogs had fought over veal cutlets from a silver basket.",
"That evening, alone with Joe, my conscience grew too heavy to carry, and I confessed that every word of it had been a lie. Joe listened patiently and then said quietly that lies were a poor sort of road, and that they never really led anywhere except further from being uncommon.",
"I lay awake a long time that night, turning over Estella's words about my coarse hands, and wondering, for the first time in my life, whether I might want to be something more than a common blacksmith's boy."
],
4:[
"Biddy, who helped Mr Wopsle's great-aunt run the evening school, began teaching me everything she knew herself, which was not a great deal but was given to me with all her heart. Slowly, letter by letter, I learned to read and write.",
"One evening at the Jolly Bargemen, a stranger sitting by the fire asked Joe about Miss Havisham and about a certain convict caught years ago on the marsh. He stirred his drink round and round with something that made my blood run cold: Joe's own file, the very one I had stolen.",
"Before he left, the stranger pressed two clean pound notes into my hand, wrapped in a scrap of paper. I understood then that somewhere out there, someone connected to my convict was still watching me, still remembering what I had done that Christmas.",
"My visits to Satis House continued, and each time I found new, greedy relatives of Miss Havisham's crowded into the house: Camilla, who complained loudly about her nerves, and Sarah Pocket, a small, dry woman with a face like a walnut shell. They all watched each other jealously, hoping for a share of the old lady's fortune.",
"In the overgrown garden one day I met a pale young gentleman who challenged me, quite politely, to fight him. I had never fought anybody in my life, but he seemed to expect it so calmly that I put up my fists and knocked him down three times before he finally gave up.",
"Afterwards, to my great astonishment, Estella allowed me to kiss her cheek, as if I had won some kind of prize. I walked home glowing with pride, though I could not have explained, even to myself, exactly what I had won.",
"For months afterwards Miss Havisham had me visit regularly to push her wheelchair round and round her overgrown garden, telling me all the while what a fine, handsome, generous man Estella would break one day.",
"Then, one afternoon, Miss Havisham asked whether Joe might come with me on my next visit. Joe put on his best coat, said almost nothing the whole time, and answered every one of Miss Havisham's questions by speaking politely to me instead of to her, which embarrassed us both terribly.",
"She gave him twenty-five guineas in a small linen bag and told him that this was Pip's premium, to make him formally her apprentice at the forge. I would learn Joe's trade after all, and my visits to Satis House were finished.",
"Mrs Joe was absolutely furious that Uncle Pumblechook, and not she, had been the one to hear the good news first, and she made everybody in the village aware of her feelings for several days afterwards.",
"Pumblechook, meanwhile, arrived at our house that same evening and took all the credit for the whole arrangement, telling everyone in the Jolly Bargemen that he, and he alone, had made a gentleman's fortune possible for a common blacksmith's boy.",
"So I became, on paper at least, a blacksmith's apprentice, bound by law to Joe for years to come, though in my secret heart I had already begun to feel that the forge could never again be enough for me."
],
5:[
"As the months passed at the forge, I grew more and more discontented with my life. I loved Joe, but I no longer wanted to be a blacksmith; I wanted, secretly and hopelessly, to be good enough for Estella.",
"Working alongside us was a journeyman named Orlick, a big, slouching, unwilling sort of workman who never seemed to think that he had done enough or been paid enough. He and Mrs Joe quarrelled constantly, each one as stubborn and as sharp-tongued as the other.",
"One particular evening their quarrel grew so loud that Joe finally lost his patience with Orlick, and the two men fought outside the forge until Mrs Joe herself begged Joe to stop hitting the wretched man.",
"Later that same night, while Joe and I were out at the Three Jolly Bargemen, somebody crept into our kitchen and struck Mrs Joe a terrible blow with a convict's iron leg-shackle, filed through and thrown away on the marsh years before. It was, everyone agreed, a savage attempt to strike her down for good.",
"Mrs Joe recovered from the blow itself, but something in her mind was broken along with her skull: she never spoke properly again, and spent her days by the fire, calm and speechless, only able to point weakly at a slate to show what she wanted.",
"Nobody was ever charged with the attack, though I always suspected, without any real proof, that Orlick's guilty face told its own story. Biddy came to live with us then, to nurse Mrs Joe and to run the house, and I was more grateful to her than I ever properly said.",
"Four quiet years went by like this, and I grew from a boy into a tall young apprentice, still ashamed of my rough hands whenever I thought of Estella. Then, one evening at the Jolly Bargemen, a stranger with a secret to tell walked in and changed my whole life.",
"A sharp, powerful stranger in city clothes introduced himself as Mr Jaggers, a London lawyer, and announced, in a loud, official voice, that he had extraordinary business with me.",
"He told me that I had great expectations: that a secret benefactor, whose name I must never try to discover, had arranged for me to receive a large fortune and to be brought up as a gentleman in London, starting immediately.",
"My head swam with excitement. It felt, I told myself proudly, exactly like a hero in a story about to inherit a kingdom, and I was certain that Miss Havisham herself must be my secret benefactor.",
"Mr Jaggers warned me sharply that I must never try to find out or even guess who my benefactor was, and that, whatever happened, I must always keep the name of Pip.",
"Within days I had new clothes made in the town, and I am ashamed now to remember how I began to patronise poor, honest Joe and gentle Biddy in those last weeks, as though becoming rich already made me better than them."
],
6:[
"Mr Jaggers's office stood in a dark, narrow street near Newgate prison, its walls hung with strange death-masks of hanged criminals, as though he wanted every visitor to remember exactly what kind of business he did.",
"His clerk, Mr Wemmick, was a dry, wooden-faced little man with a mouth exactly like a post-office slot: he seemed to open it only to receive letters, never to smile.",
"My new lodgings were at Barnard's Inn, a shabby, comfortable set of rooms shared with none other than the pale young gentleman I had once knocked down in Miss Havisham's garden: Herbert Pocket.",
"Herbert laughed about our old fight without the slightest resentment, decided that Pip would never do for a gentleman's friend, and began calling me Handel instead, after a piece of music he liked.",
"One evening he told me the whole sad story of Miss Havisham: years before, she had been engaged to marry a smooth, plausible swindler named Compeyson, who had worked together with Arthur, her own half-brother, to trick her out of a large part of her fortune.",
"On the very morning of the wedding, with the cake already cut and the guests already arriving, Compeyson had simply vanished, leaving a cruel letter behind him. Everyone in the district still called her the woman Compeyson had jilted, and she had stopped every clock in the house at the exact moment she read it.",
"By day I studied, not very seriously, with Herbert's own father, Mr Matthew Pocket, at his crowded, chaotic house in Hammersmith, alongside two other students: Bentley Drummle, a rich, sulky young man who seemed to dislike everybody equally, and cheerful, easy-going Startop.",
"Wemmick, so cold and businesslike at the office, turned out to have a completely different side to him. At his tiny wooden house in Walworth, which he proudly called his Castle, complete with a small cannon and a drawbridge, he lived happily with his deaf old father, whom he called simply the Aged.",
"“A man needs somewhere the law and the office can't follow him,” Wemmick told me firmly, nodding towards his little cannon. “Out here I'm simply myself. Jaggers never sees this side of me, and I mean to keep it that way.”",
"I dined once at Mr Jaggers's own respectable, gloomy house, where a quiet, watchful housekeeper called Molly served the meal. Jaggers, half showing off and half warning us, took her wrist and turned it over to show us the strange, deep scars across it, though he refused to explain how she had got them.",
"Everybody at that dinner was too polite, or too frightened of Jaggers, to ask any more about Molly, though I could not stop thinking about those scars for days afterwards.",
"I began to understand, slowly, that London was full of people with secrets kept as carefully as any prisoner behind a locked door, and that I, with a fortune I could not explain, was quickly becoming one of them."
],
7:[
"One morning a letter arrived to say that Joe himself was coming up to London to see me. I am ashamed, even now, of how my heart sank instead of leaping at the news.",
"He arrived at Barnard's Inn in his very best Sunday clothes, stiff and awkward in them, holding his hat in both hands like a nervous schoolboy instead of a grown blacksmith. I found myself embarrassed by every one of his simple, kind old habits at my elegant new table.",
"Joe noticed my coldness, of course; he noticed everything important, whatever people thought of him. Before he left he took my hand and said, quietly and with a dignified sort of sadness, that a blacksmith and a gentleman could never really be comfortable together outside of the old forge, and that he would not come again unless I sent for him.",
"I went home myself soon afterwards and found, to my surprise, that Orlick was now working as Miss Havisham's gatekeeper at Satis House, letting visitors in and out through the same gate where I had once waited as a boy.",
"Estella had grown into a tall, graceful young woman, more beautiful and more coldly perfect than I had ever imagined her, and my old childish feelings turned, almost instantly, into something much more painful.",
"Miss Havisham watched us together with a strange, hungry excitement, gripping my arm and insisting, over and over, that I must give Estella my whole heart, no matter how coldly she treated it in return.",
"Back in London I finally admitted the truth to Herbert: that I loved Estella completely and hopelessly, and had loved her since the very first afternoon at Satis House. Herbert listened kindly, and then, to my astonishment, admitted a secret of his own — that he was engaged to a gentle girl called Clara, though neither of their fathers yet knew of it.",
"One evening we went together to see Mr Wopsle perform in an absolutely absurd production of Hamlet, in which the whole audience laughed loudly at every serious, tragic moment, until poor Wopsle looked almost as unhappy as the Danish prince himself.",
"Some weeks later I was asked to meet Estella's coach when it arrived in London and to take her out to Richmond, where she was going to live with a fashionable lady. I felt prouder walking beside her that afternoon than I had ever felt about anything in my whole life.",
"On the journey she warned me, calmly and without any unkindness in her cold, level voice, that I must never expect too much from her, because she had, she said, no heart at all to give to anybody. I told myself she could not possibly mean it, though everyone who knew her called her heartless, and I was slowly beginning to believe them.",
"I felt myself blush and my throat tighten, but I said nothing, because I could not risk losing even the little of her that I already had.",
"I left her at Richmond that evening more in love, and more without hope, than I had ever been before, carrying her warning inside me like a stone I could not put down."
],
8:[
"Herbert and I lived, in those years, rather more expensively than either of us could really afford, running up debts we hardly dared to add together, and joining a foolish, expensive dining club called the Finches of the Grove, whose only real purpose seemed to be spending money.",
"Word came, quietly and suddenly, that Mrs Joe had died in her sleep. I went home for the funeral feeling more guilt than grief, since I had visited her so rarely since becoming a gentleman.",
"Standing by the grave, I promised Biddy that I would come and see her often. She looked at me steadily and said, with a small, sad smile, that she did not quite believe me, and, though it stung, I understood exactly why.",
"When I turned twenty-one, Mr Jaggers informed me that I would now receive five hundred pounds a year from my benefactor. With most of this money I did something I had wanted to do for a long time: I arranged, through Wemmick and completely without Herbert's knowledge, an anonymous partnership for him with a young merchant called Clarriker.",
"Herbert never discovered, for years, exactly who his mysterious patron was, and I never told him, because his happiness that day was worth more to me than any thanks he might have given me.",
"Down at Richmond, meanwhile, a rich, sulky young man called Bentley Drummle had begun to court Estella seriously, following her everywhere with the patient stupidity of a large, well-fed dog.",
"I visited Satis House once during this time and overheard Miss Havisham and Estella quarrelling bitterly: Miss Havisham accusing Estella of being cold and unloving even towards her, and Estella answering, quite calmly, that she was only what Miss Havisham herself had made her.",
"One wild, stormy night, when I was twenty-three, thunder rolled continuously over London and rain lashed against the windows of my rooms at the Temple. I was reading alone by candlelight when I heard slow, heavy footsteps climbing the staircase towards my door.",
"A ragged, weather-worn man of about sixty stood in the doorway, his hands rough and scarred, his clothes rough sailor's clothes, and something in his strange, searching face that seemed both familiar and completely unknown to me.",
"He held out both his hands to me with a kind of desperate warmth, and told me, in a voice I suddenly remembered from twelve years before on a dark, freezing marsh, that he had been transported to Australia after his capture, had made a considerable fortune raising sheep, and had come back to England, at enormous risk to his own life, to see the gentleman he had made.",
"I stared at him, utterly astonished, unable at first to understand a single word he was saying, until the terrible truth broke over me all at once: this ragged convict, and not Miss Havisham, was my secret benefactor.",
"I felt nothing but horror. Every hope I had quietly built for myself, that Miss Havisham intended me for Estella, collapsed in that one stormy hour, and I stood there, horrified and speechless, in front of the very man whose escape I had helped, all those years ago, on the cold December marsh."
],
9:[
"I could not let Magwitch simply walk the streets of London, where any policeman might recognise an escaped convict who had returned from transportation, a crime that carried the punishment of death. Herbert and I found him quiet lodgings on Essex Street, where we could hide him under the safe, ordinary alias of Mr Provis.",
"Every day he stayed in London he was in terrible danger, and every day I felt the danger too, since helping an escaped convict was itself a dangerous crime.",
"One evening, sitting close by the fire so that his low, rough voice would not carry through the walls, Magwitch told Herbert and me the whole story of his life, from its very beginning.",
"He had grown up in complete poverty, he said, with no family that he could properly remember, moving from one prison and one workhouse to another almost since he could walk. Nobody had ever once been kind to him without wanting something in return.",
"As a young man he had fallen in with a smooth, well-spoken gentleman named Compeyson, who dressed well, spoke well, and used Magwitch's rough hands to do all the dangerous, dirty work of their swindles together.",
"When the two of them were finally caught and tried for the same crime, Compeyson's gentleman's manners and expensive lawyer won him a far lighter sentence than Magwitch's, though Magwitch always insisted that Compeyson had planned every single unfair scheme they carried out.",
"“It was him wot got the light sentence, and me wot got the heavy one, though he'd led the whole business from the start,” Magwitch said bitterly, his old anger at the trial still burning after so many years.",
"Herbert and I looked at each other in astonishment, for we both now understood, with a shock, that Compeyson was the very same swindler who had abandoned Miss Havisham on her wedding morning, and that our two separate stories had been connected all along.",
"Some time later, at the Blue Boar near Satis House, I overheard Bentley Drummle boasting loudly about Estella and how completely he expected to win her, and I felt sick with jealousy that I could not risk showing.",
"I understood now, with painful clarity, that I could not honestly take one more shilling of Magwitch's dangerous, hard-earned fortune, since keeping it meant risking his life every single day he remained near me in England.",
"Herbert and I resolved instead, quietly and carefully, to plan a rescue of a different kind: to get Magwitch safely out of England on a foreign steamer before Compeyson, who I feared was somewhere near, could find him and betray him for the reward.",
"For the first time since that stormy night, I felt something more than horror towards the ragged man who called himself my second father: something close, though I could hardly admit it yet, to guilt for my own coldness, and to a strange, growing loyalty."
],
10:[
"I went to Satis House one last time and, without meaning to accuse her cruelly, told Miss Havisham that she had used me unfairly, letting me believe for years that she herself was raising me for some purpose connected with Estella and her fortune.",
"I did not ask her to forgive herself for any of it. I asked her only one thing for myself: to finish, quietly and secretly, the small sum still owing on Herbert's partnership, which I had begun to arrange before I fully understood how little money I could honestly call my own. She agreed at once, almost eagerly, as though the request pleased her.",
"Then Estella walked in, cold and beautiful as always, and announced, without the smallest warmth in her voice, that she had decided to marry Bentley Drummle.",
"I poured out my whole heart to her then, telling her that I had loved her since I was a frightened boy with coarse hands in that very garden, and that I always would, whatever she did or said.",
"She listened to it all with a kind of patient, distant pity, and told me plainly that she did not reject me out of unkindness, but simply because there was nothing in her to give any man, least of all a man as good as I was.",
"Back in London a warning note from Wemmick reached me with three simple, frightening words: “Don't go home.” Somebody, he had learned, was watching my rooms, and Compeyson, the swindler from Magwitch's story, was somewhere in the city.",
"Herbert and I moved Magwitch at once, downstream along the river, to lodgings at Clara's house on Mill Pond Bank, a quiet spot from which we might one day row him out to a foreign steamer without being noticed.",
"Every free evening after that we practised rowing on the river, disguising our real purpose as simple exercise, so that our little boat and our faces would become a familiar, harmless sight along that stretch of water before the night we would truly need it.",
"One evening at the theatre, watching poor Wopsle attempt another hopeless part, he leaned across to me during the interval and said, quite calmly, that a strange man had been sitting directly behind me all evening: a man he was almost certain he recognised from years before, on trial with a convict called Magwitch.",
"My blood ran cold. I understood at once, and I realised with a shock that left me sick, that Compeyson himself had been sitting close enough to touch me, watching me without my ever once turning round.",
"At a strange, tense dinner at Jaggers's house not long afterwards, I found myself studying Molly's quiet, watchful face and her strong, moving hands as she poured our wine, and slowly, piece by piece, I began to see in them an unmistakable resemblance to somebody else entirely.",
"It was Estella's own eyes I was looking into, and Estella's own steady hands, and I understood, with an astonishment greater than almost anything else in this whole strange story, that the housekeeper serving our dinner was Estella's true mother."
],
11:[
"Full of remorse, Miss Havisham handed me a heavy pocket-book with the exact sum needed to finish paying for Herbert's partnership, and then, to my complete astonishment, fell on her knees in front of me, begging my forgiveness for the way she had used my heart to feed Estella's coldness.",
"I lifted her gently, telling her I forgave her freely, though my own heart was still raw from Estella's rejection. As I turned to leave, I glanced back and saw a sudden flame leap up from the fireplace and catch the edge of her ancient wedding dress.",
"In a second she was almost entirely on fire, screaming and running wildly, and I threw myself at her, pulling the tablecloth and my own coat around her to smother the flames, though not before my hands and arms were badly burned in the struggle.",
"Miss Havisham survived that terrible night, though badly hurt, and lay afterwards in a strange, gentle state, repeating my name and asking again and again for my forgiveness, until I found I truly meant it when I gave it.",
"Back in London, while my hands healed slowly under bandages, Herbert told me something Magwitch had once mentioned to him alone: years before, Magwitch had had a wife, a jealous, dangerous woman, and a small daughter he believed had died as a baby.",
"A sudden, extraordinary suspicion formed in my mind, and I went at once to Mr Jaggers and asked him, as directly as I dared, about the child. After a long silence, he finally admitted, in his own careful, lawyer's way, that he himself had placed a certain little orphan girl with a rich, grieving lady many years before. Though he never said it plainly, it was, in every way that mattered, a full reveal of what I already suspected.",
"Estella, cold, beautiful Estella, was the very child of the ragged convict who called himself my second father, and I understood then how strangely and completely all the threads of this story had been tied together from the very beginning.",
"Not long after this, I received an unsigned note asking me to come alone, that same evening, to the old sluice-house by the abandoned lime-kiln on the marshes, on business connected with Provis.",
"Foolishly trusting the note, I went alone into the darkness and found, waiting for me inside, not a friend but Orlick: I had walked straight into his trap, and his eyes were full of an old, drunken hatred I had never fully understood before that night.",
"He had lured me there deliberately, he told me with horrible satisfaction, tying my arms tightly to a ladder before I could properly defend myself, and then, almost proudly, he confessed that it was he, and not some unknown stranger, who had struck down Mrs Joe all those years before.",
"He was working himself up, desperate and half mad with drink, towards killing me too, when suddenly the door burst open and Herbert, Startop and young Trabb's boy rushed in with a lantern, arriving in the very last possible moment to save my life.",
"Orlick escaped into the darkness of the marsh before anyone could catch him, but I never forgot how close I had come to dying there, nor how loyal my true friends had been to follow me out onto that lonely marsh for my rescue in the middle of the night."
],
12:[
"The night of the escape came at last. Herbert, Startop and I rowed Magwitch quietly down the dark river towards a foreign steamer that would carry him safely away from England forever.",
"Everything went smoothly until, quite suddenly, a police galley appeared out of the darkness ahead of us, moving to intercept our little boat, with Compeyson himself standing in the bow, pointing directly at Magwitch.",
"Magwitch threw himself across the water at his old enemy before the soldiers could act, and the two men struggled together beneath the surface for a long, terrible moment while our boat rocked wildly in the current.",
"Only Magwitch came back up. Compeyson, weighed down by his own fear and fury, drowned somewhere in the dark water beneath us, and was never properly recovered from the river.",
"Magwitch himself was pulled from the water badly hurt, with a wound on his chest and a broken rib, and was captured at last by the soldiers he had spent his whole life trying to avoid.",
"Because a convict's property automatically became the property of the Crown, every shilling of Magwitch's hard-earned fortune was taken from him at once, and I found, to my own surprise, that I did not care about the money at all any more.",
"I visited him every single day after that in the prison hospital, sitting quietly by his bed as his health grew steadily worse through the autumn.",
"At his trial he was found guilty and sentenced to death, though he was already too ill for the sentence ever to be carried out. He grew weaker and weaker, understanding little around him except my own face and my own hand in his.",
"In his last hours I leaned close and told him, gently, that the daughter he believed he had lost so long ago was alive, and grown into a beautiful woman, and that I loved her dearly. A kind of peace came over his rough, tired face, and he died holding my hand.",
"Soon afterwards I fell seriously ill myself, worn out by grief and by months of danger and debt, and for many feverish days I hardly knew where I was. When I finally woke properly, it was Joe's steady, gentle face I saw beside my bed, not a stranger's.",
"Joe had nursed me patiently through the worst of my illness, quietly paid off every one of my remaining debts without ever once mentioning it, and then, feeling that a gentleman no longer needed a blacksmith's care, slipped away home before I could properly thank him.",
"As soon as I was strong enough I hurried down to the forge to ask Biddy to marry me, only to arrive on the very morning of her wedding, dressed in her best, and holding Joe's arm: for it was Joe, gentle and faithful Joe, that she was marrying, not me.",
"I went abroad with Herbert for eleven years, working hard and honestly in his firm, and slowly built the kind of ordinary, useful life I might never have imagined for myself as a boy at the forge.",
"When I finally returned to England, older and quieter, I walked once more through the ruined, overgrown garden of Satis House, now falling gently into decay, and found Estella there too, widowed now and softened by loss into someone gentler and more real than the cold girl I had once loved so hopelessly.",
"We stood together in the last evening light among the broken walls and the wild grass where the old house had once stood, and this time, when I held out my hand, Estella took it, softened at last, and we walked out of the ruined garden together, hand in hand, ready at last to begin again."
]
};
